INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l01', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Signing up and the setup chat', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya edits videos in Pune. She posts her own Reels, and she edits for brands on the side. Today she opens kreators.app for the first time. In a few taps the app should know who she is and switch on what fits her.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How a new person gets from Welcome to Home.
- What the account card and the privacy card ask, and why.
- How devices and passwords work.
- What you, as an admin, can check and change.

## Step one: Welcome

Riya taps Start free on the site. That opens Welcome. It says "Seven relationships. One you." and shows one big button.

1. She taps **Start with a short chat**.
2. The app opens a session on her phone, so nothing she says is lost.
3. The setup chat opens with Bumling, the app''s own guide.

If she stops halfway, the button says **Carry on setting up** next time. The line under it says her answers are saved on this device.

## Step two: the account card

Bumling asks for the account first. The card has four fields.

1. **Your name:** Riya types "Riya".
2. **Email:** her work email.
3. **Password:** eight or more letters, numbers or signs. It cannot be her email or a common one.
4. **Password again:** the same one, so a typo cannot lock her out.

She taps **Next**. The eye button inside each password field shows or hides what she typed.

## Step three: the private card

Next comes a second, small card with two private details.

- **Phone:** she types her ten-digit number. A number with no country code is read as Indian and gets +91.
- **Date of birth:** you must be 18 or older to join. Nobody else ever sees it.
- **The terms box:** "I agree to the Terms of use and have read the Privacy policy." It starts unticked. She must tick it herself.

She taps **Make my account**. The chat says she is in.

## Step four: the few questions

Now Bumling asks a few quick questions. Each list comes in small groups of chips. Riya taps what fits and then **Next**.

1. **What she wants:** up to three goals. Riya picks "Earn from my content", "Manage my clients and invoices" and "Plan my week and stay on track".
2. **Who she is:** she picks "I make content" and "I freelance for clients".
3. **What she makes:** since she does both, she gets two groups, "Your own content" and "For clients". She picks "Reels and Shorts" and "Editing".
4. **What gets in the way:** she picks "Not enough time".

After each answer, Bumling says it back in plain words. Under every question sit **Tell me more** and **Change my last answer**. The typing box works at any time. Riya types "what is Army?" and gets a plain answer. Then her choices come back.

## Step five: the apps

Bumling shows a short list of apps, each with one line. Since Riya said time is tight, the list is short on purpose. **Why these apps?** shows the reason for each one. She could untick any app she does not want. She keeps all three and taps **Switch on 3 apps**. Every other app the chat can switch is turned off, and she can turn any of them on later.

The last card offers a few next steps: **Go to Home**, the strategy session, the Discover quiz, and **Add my interests**. Riya picks Go to Home.

> **Pro tip:** tell new people they can run this again any time. On the Profile tab, **Change my tags and apps** opens the same chat with their last answers filled in.

## Step six: the privacy card

A small card called **Your privacy** sits at the bottom left. It asks about three things, and all three start off.

- **Remember which link brought me:** a cookie for thirty days that tells a creator which tracked link sent you.
- **Show videos from YouTube and Vimeo:** off means a video waits for a tap.
- **Send me news and offers by email:** off means only the mail you need, like receipts.

Riya can tap **Reject all** or **Accept all**. They look the same, so neither is pushed. **Choose for each** opens the three switches and a **Save my choices** button. She can change them later on the Profile tab, under Privacy choices.

## Devices and passwords

- **Three devices at once:** an account can be signed in on three devices. A fourth signs out the oldest one. That device''s sign-in screen then says why.
- **Change password:** on the Profile tab, under Account, Riya taps **Change password**. She types the current one, then the new one twice. Every other device is signed out.
- **Forgot it:** the sign-in screen has **Forgot your password?** It mails a one-time link that works for thirty minutes.

> **Result:** Riya has an account, her tags, her apps and her privacy choices. Home is open.

## What an admin checks

Open **Admin** from the Operations group on Apps. Only admins see it.

1. Go to the **People** tab. Search by name, email, page or id.
2. Find Riya''s row. You see her role, plan, credits, xp and points. You never see her password, phone or date of birth.
3. Tap her row. You can give permissions, honors, points, xp, badges and a plan.
4. Under **What does this person see?** tick or untick an app for her alone. **Reset** brings the usual rules back.
5. A wrong sign-up can be removed with the delete icon at the end of the row. It asks first in a sheet. An admin account, or your own, cannot be deleted there.

A few more things to know:

- **Founder emails:** an address on the admin list gets the admin role at sign-up. Sign up with yours before anyone else can.
- **Alerts:** many failed sign-ins in a short time send a note to every admin''s inbox, at most once an hour.
- **Logs:** a super admin can read refusals and failed sign-ins under Logs. No message bodies are kept there.
- **Audit:** every role change and hand-given xp shows on the **Audit** tab.

## The bottom line

Sign-up is one chat. Two small cards make the account, a few taps pick the apps, and privacy starts off. As an admin, you check people in People and leave their private details alone.', 1, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l02', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Pillars and the strategy session', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya has her apps. But apps alone do not tell her where her life needs work. The Pillars tab does. It holds the seven relationships of her life, and the strategy session sets them up in one sitting.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya runs the strategy session, part by part.
- What the pyramid is, and its six layers.
- Where the life map, the routine and the contract live after.
- What an admin can and cannot see.

## The seven pillars

Riya opens the **Pillars** tab. It reads "Your seven pillars". Each pillar is one relationship: Servitude, Friends, Family, Love, Yourself, Teachers and Faith. Each has its own guide, like Ember for Servitude and Dew for Yourself.

Before the session, a dark card sits near the top: **Set up your pillars: the strategy session**. She taps it.

## Step one: the map

The session is one chat, hosted by Bumling. First comes the map.

1. A row of seven faces sits at the top, one per relationship.
2. For each one, Riya ticks every feeling she knows from it, on good days and bad ones.
3. The feelings come in three groups: **Stuck**, **Owning it** and **At ease**.
4. Love, Teachers or Faith can be set aside with **Not part of my life right now**.
5. If ticking is hard, **Say it in your own words** opens a box. **Read my words** suggests feelings for free. **Read with AI** shows only with a plan.
6. She taps **That is my map**.

From her ticks, the app works out where she usually is and where she drops under stress. The key line is **Courage**. A relationship that drops below it is a good place to start. If she places one at Inner light or higher, she is asked for one thing she did last week that shows it.

## Step two: what gets in the way

Next she names fears and insecurities. A fear shows up when she is about to act. An insecurity is what she worries others notice. She picks the relationship each one touches, then taps **That is what gets in the way**. Sending it empty is fine.

## Step three: the scorecard and her real day

1. She rates her health, her wealth and each relationship from one to five stars, then taps **Those are my stars**.
2. If a high score does not match her map, one quick question asks how many days last week she acted with courage there.
3. She fills in a normal day as it is now: **Wake up**, **Lights out**, **Work starts**, **Work ends** and a few more. Then **That is my day**.

## Step four: focus, routine and practices

1. Bumling shows her map and suggests where to start. Riya keeps the picks or chooses her own, up to two, and taps **Work on these**.
2. Her routine appears. It has six blocks, built from her real day. Only the morning has steps to tick. She taps **Looks right**.
3. Lumen, guide to faith, asks for her morning practices: a mantra, a breathing exercise, a line to say and something to read. **Those are my practices**.

For every week she keeps the routine on five days, her wake-up time moves thirty minutes earlier, until half past six.

## Step five: the contract

The contract turns the plan into a promise.

- **Days a week, at least:** five to start with.
- **Daily check-in time:** a reminder lands in her inbox at that time on any day she has not checked in.
- **When a week falls short:** The streak breaks, Unlocks wait, or The coach sees it.
- **Type your name to sign:** she types "Riya" and taps **Sign it**.

A switch offers a live call with a coach, for ₹10,000. Booking opens once payments are connected. Saying no changes nothing.

## Step six: the pyramids

Each focus pillar gets a pyramid. A pyramid has six layers, from the top down:

1. **Foundation:** one thing she will not let break, ever.
2. **Guiding principle:** a line she lives by, so the foundation holds.
3. **Value goal:** how she wants to feel more often.
4. **Goal outcome:** what she would see when the value goal is real.
5. **Project:** a bunch of tasks with a finish line.
6. **Task:** the point at the bottom, one small thing she can tick today.

Each pillar''s guide asks three quick questions. **Write my own** lets her answer in her own words. Then she picks a value goal and a project for each pillar and taps **Build my pillars**. The chat ends with **Open my pillars** or **Add my interests**.

> **Pro tip:** a person who skips the session can still build a pillar by hand. On a pillar''s page, **Set up this pillar** offers the guide''s ready pyramid, or **Or start by hand with a foundation**.

## After the session

- **Pillar cards:** each card on Pillars now shows where that relationship sits on the map.
- **A pillar''s page:** **Tasks to do** with **Add a task**, then **The pyramid**, where each layer opens to change it or add to it.
- **The life map:** at the bottom of Pillars, under "Your life map". It holds Month one, Where each one sits, Part of your life?, The whole map, Day zero scores, What gets in the way, Your routine and The contract.
- **Your routine:** she can change any block''s times, add or remove morning steps and set their minutes.
- **Again later:** **Run the session again** and **Build the pillars for your focus** sit at the top of the life map.

> **Result:** Riya has a map, one or two focus pillars, a routine, a signed contract and her first pyramids. Her first tasks will show on Daily.

## What an admin checks

The strategy session and Pillars are core. You cannot switch them off for anyone, and they are always open.

1. **Privacy:** her map, scores, fears and contract are hers. No admin screen shows them.
2. **Coaches:** a life coach sees only what Riya shares on that link. There are five switches, including Pillars and pyramids, Routine and contract, and Life map answers. She can turn any off.
3. **Making a coach:** use the **Life coaches** tab in Admin to answer people who asked to coach.
4. **The live call:** until payments are live, a person asks under Help, and the team sets a time by hand.

## The bottom line

Pillars is the heart of the app. One session gives Riya her map, her routine, her contract and her pyramids. As an admin, you keep it open, make coaches carefully, and never look inside.', 2, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l03', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Daily: the boxes, the tasks and the week', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya''s pillars are built. Now she needs one place to see what to do today. That is the Daily tab. It is where her plan turns into ticks.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya checks in and does her small act.
- How she plans a task: do date, due date, repeat, priority and area of life.
- How the month calendar, Plan my week and time blocks help.
- How the not to do list and Habits work, and what an admin can do.

## Step one: show up

At the top of Daily is **Show up**.

1. Riya taps **I showed up today**. That is her check-in. It pays 10 xp and 10 points and keeps her streak. Every seventh day in a row pays a bonus of 50 points.
2. Under it is her morning routine from the strategy session. She ticks each step. A day counts only when every morning step is ticked.

## Step two: the small act

Next is her guide''s small act for today, like "Dew''s small act for today". It is one two-minute thing for her weakest pillar. Ticking it pays 15 xp.

## Step three: tasks

Under **Tasks** sit the tasks from her pillars. A task pays 20 xp when ticked. Her first three ticks have no wait. After that, a task must be ten minutes old and ten minutes after her last tick.

To plan one, Riya taps the pencil beside it. The plan opens right under the task.

1. **Do on:** the do date. The task shows on Daily from that day, and cannot be ticked before. **Tomorrow** and **Next week** are quick picks.
2. **Due by:** the deadline. After it the task shows Overdue, but it never stops a tick.
3. **Repeat:** Every day, Weekdays, Every week, Every month or Every year, every one or more. She can pick the days of the week and a **Stop after** day.
4. **Count it as a habit:** shows while a repeat is set, and is on to start. The task then wears the Habit tag.
5. **Priority:** P0 Do now, P1 Important, P2 Normal or P3 Whenever. It sorts the list and never changes what a tick pays.
6. **Area of life:** No area, or Physical, Financial, Mental, Emotional or Spiritual. The task wears that tag, and a tick counts toward that area in Life.
7. **Remember:** a tag. With a day, one note lands in her inbox on that day, at the task''s time, or at 9:00 when it has none.
8. **Bring it to my review:** the task waits for her next review.
9. **Time** and **Minutes:** when she will do it and how long it should take.

She taps **Save plan**. With minutes set, **Start focus** opens Mindfulness with the task picked and the timer set to that length.

Riya plans "Edit the brand reel": do date Thursday, due Friday, P1, Financial, 90 minutes.

> **Pro tip:** the do date and the due date are not the same. The do date is when she will work on it. The due date is when it must be done. The do date cannot come after the due date.

## Step four: sort and kind of day

- **Kind of day:** a dropdown of her day templates. It starts on **Follow your week**, with the template her week gives that day, like Everyday. Riya picks Deep work day on edit days, and only that date changes. The next lesson covers day templates and her usual week.
- **Area:** once a task has an area, a filter shows every area, one area, or No area.
- **Sort by:** Day, Priority, Due date, By context, By time block or By area. By context keeps P0 first, and inside each priority puts the pillars her day template leads with first.
- **By time block** and **By area** draw her tasks in coloured boxes that open and fold, with P0 first in each box.

## Step five: the month and the week

1. **Month calendar** opens a month grid. Each day shows how many tasks are left. She taps a day to see its tasks, or to **Add a task on this day**. Each day wears its kind of day''s colour, and in a day''s sheet she can set that date''s Kind of day, past or future, or pick Follow your week to clear it. **Print this month** gives a clean paper page.
2. **Add a task**, under the list, asks "What is the task?" and "Which project does it hold up?".
3. **Plan my week**, beside the Tasks heading, gives each task with no day a suggested day in the next seven, and a time block. She sets **Hours of planned work a day**. **Worth a look** lists problems, each with a **Fix**. **Add your own** finds any open task. Nothing changes until she taps **Keep what fits**.
4. **Time blocks**, beside Plan my week, opens the page where she shapes her days. On Daily, the **Your day** fold shows each block of her day as a band, with its tasks inside, the free time left and any block that runs over.

## Step six: the lists below

- **To remember:** Remember tasks with no day yet. She picks a day to move one into Tasks.
- **Not scheduled yet:** tasks with no do date and no due date. They never join the main list until she picks a day.
- **Done today:** what she ticked, struck through with the time. Tapping one unticks it and takes back its xp.

## Step seven: not to do and Habits

**Not to do** is the list of things she stays away from, up to seven. Riya adds "No phone in bed" and taps **Add**. Each day she ticks the ones she kept. A kept day pays 5 xp and grows the streak. The three-dot button opens **I slipped today** and **Remove from the list**.

**Habits** sits under it. When a task she ticked on three or more days in eight weeks lines up with better or worse days, it shows as a **Good habit** or a **Bad habit**. A line says what went together, never what caused what. A bad one can go to the not to do list.

> **Result:** Riya opens Daily, ticks the check-in, her routine, her small act and her edit task. Her week is planned, and her phone stays out of bed.

## What an admin checks

Daily is core and always on. You cannot switch it off for anyone.

1. **Privacy:** her tasks, not to do list and habits are hers. No admin screen lists them.
2. **Coaches:** a coach sees Daily and streaks only if Riya shares it on their link.
3. **XP by hand:** in **Admin**, **People**, tap her row. Under **XP**, add or take away xp with a note. The note and your name go to the audit log.
4. **Reviews:** Reviews starts off. Riya turns it on herself under **Apps** (Reflection). **What does this person see?** only allows or blocks an app; it never switches one on for her.
5. **Wait and cap:** after her first three ticks, a task must be ten minutes old and ten minutes after her last tick, and only six ticks a day pay xp. These guard against people gaming the boxes.

## The bottom line

Daily is three kinds of boxes and one list of tasks. A do date says when, a due date says by when, and the plan says the rest. As an admin, you keep its rules fair and leave the tasks to their owner.', 3, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l24', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Time blocks: the shape of a member''s day', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya''s tasks have dates now. But a date does not say when in the day. Time blocks give her day a shape: Deep work in the morning, Recovery after lunch, Sleep at night. Her tasks then fall into those blocks.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What Riya sets up: blocks, day templates, her usual week and one-day changes.
- How her tasks fall into blocks, and how Daily and Plan my week use them.
- What emerges from it, and what stays private.
- What to check when a member asks for help, and why nothing here is staff-only.

## Where it lives

Time blocks is a page under Daily, not an app. It is core, like Daily, so it is always on. It is not on Apps, and it is not in **What does this person see?**. Nobody can switch it off, and no admin screen lists what is on it.

Riya reaches it three ways:

1. **Time blocks**, beside **Plan my week** at the Tasks heading on Daily.
2. **Edit kind of day**, the small link under Daily''s sort row.
3. The gear at the top of Plan my week.

## Step one: the starting set

The first time Riya opens Daily or the page, the app gives her a starting set, once:

- **14 blocks.** Ten make up a normal day, from Morning hygiene at 7 am to Sleep at 11 pm. Travel, Family time, Date and Reflection are used by some templates.
- **14 day templates.** Everyday is the default. The rest are Deep work day, Create day, Learning day, Money day, Admin day, Family day, Recovery day, Social day, Date day, Reflection day, Low energy day, Self love day and Travel day.

Every one is hers to change. The page says so above the templates: "The starting ones are yours to change."

## Step two: her blocks

A block has a name, a tag, a colour and, if she likes, an area of life and a pillar. Under **Blocks**, Riya taps Deep work and changes it.

1. **Tag:** a word or two, like Focus, Admin, Self care or Recovery. She can type her own.
2. **Colour:** eleven colours round a wheel, from amber to lime. The twelfth button, the pipette, opens a small card for her own colour. She types a hex code like 1E90FF, or a colour name like teal, and taps the tick.
3. **Area of life** and **Pillar:** tasks in that area, or of that pillar, are suggested for this block.
4. She taps **Save**.

**Add a block** makes a new one, and **Arrange** changes the order. Deleting a block asks first. Her tasks stay; they only lose the block. She can keep up to 24 blocks.

## Step three: her day templates

A day template lays blocks on the clock, with the pillar that leads and a second pillar. It is her kind of day. Riya opens Deep work day under **Day templates**.

1. Each slot is a block with a start and an end. An end before the start runs past midnight, like Sleep from 11 pm to 7 am.
2. Slots never overlap. If two do, a line names both, and **Save** waits until she fixes it. A gap shows as free time.
3. **Save** keeps the change. **Save as new** keeps a copy instead.
4. The three dots hold **Make it the default**, **Copy** and **Delete**.

She can keep up to 24 templates, each with up to 24 slots. **New template** copies the day she is looking at as "My day".

## Step four: her usual week, and one-day changes

Every day starts on her default, Everyday. Under **Your usual week**, Riya opens **Set each weekday** and gives Sunday Family day. Each change saves at once.

Some days do not fit. For one date only, she picks another template in **Kind of day**, on Daily or under the day she taps in **This week**, or in the month calendar on Daily: she taps any day, past or future, and picks its Kind of day. A changed day wears a **Changed** tag, and her week stays as it was. **Use it on more days** puts a template on other weekdays, **Every week** or **This week only**. **Follow your week** undoes a one-day change.

## Step five: tasks in blocks

A task finds its block in one of three ways:

1. She picks it in the task''s plan, under **Time block**.
2. Left on **By its time**, a task sits in the block its time falls in.
3. Plan my week suggests one, from the task''s area of life, else its pillar. P0 and P1 lean to a block tagged Focus. P2 and P3 lean to the others.

On Daily, **Sort by** has **By time block** and **By area**. Each draws her tasks in coloured boxes, up to 60 tasks, with P0 first in every box. **By context** reads the pillar her day template leads with. **Your day**, a fold above the list, shows each block as a band with "45 min free" or "Over by 30 min".

In Plan my week, each suggestion shows its block, with a select to change it. **Worth a look** says when a block holds too much on a day, or when a Focus block is empty. Nothing changes until she taps **Keep what fits**.

> **Pro tip:** when a member says Daily feels like a wall of tasks, point them to **By time block**. Seeing the day as boxes often does more than another rule.

## What emerges

After some weeks, Emerging on Daily reads her blocks back to her. It may say "You ticked 14 of 20 Deep work tasks inside the block." It also sets the tasks she ticks on one kind of day beside her other days.

**Your kinds of day** shows her last four weeks by kind, each in the colour of its longest block, and how each kind went beside her other days: tasks ticked, and focus minutes and mood while those apps are on. She sees it on Daily, on the Time blocks page, under Emerging and on Reviews. A day counts as the kind set for that date, else the one her week gives it. Days on Everyday count as no kind. Reviews also has a **Kinds of day** tile for the period, and **This month** on the Time blocks page counts her days of each kind so far. Every line says what went together, never what caused what.

> **Result:** Riya''s Monday is a Deep work day, her Sunday a Family day, and her tasks sit in the blocks where they fit. When a trip comes up, she changes one date and her week stays whole.

## What an admin checks

Nothing here is staff-only. There is no admin screen for blocks, templates or the week, no switch, and no override. When a member asks, you guide them on their own screen.

1. **"My starting blocks are gone."** At the foot of the page, **Bring back the starting set** adds back any starting block or template they deleted. Their own stay as they are.
2. **"I can''t delete a template."** It is the default. They make another one the default first.
3. **"I can''t add a block."** They have 24, the most anyone can keep. Templates stop at 24 too.
4. **"A slot won''t save."** Two slots overlap. The line under them names both.
5. **"A task is in the wrong block."** A block picked in the task''s plan wins over its time. They set it back to **By its time**.
6. **"My Everyday days are not counted."** Everyday is the default, and a day on it counts as no kind. They give a day a kind on Daily or in the month calendar, or set a weekday''s template in their usual week.
7. **"Daily sorts in an odd order."** On a day that follows the default, Daily sorts by day. A changed date or another weekday template sorts By context. Any sort they pick stays theirs.
8. **The assistant:** with a plan, it can read their blocks and set a day''s template for them, each with Undo.
9. **Privacy:** a coach never sees blocks or templates. The Logs show that a change was made, never which template.
10. **Their data:** blocks, templates and the week are in their export. **Erase everything** keeps them, since they are settings, not progress. Deleting the account removes them.

## The bottom line

Time blocks give a member''s day a shape, and a day template is their kind of day. It is theirs from end to end. As an admin, you know where each button is, so you can guide them, and you leave the blocks to their owner.', 4, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l04', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Home: your day at a glance', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya opens kreators.app on a busy morning. She has two edits due and a Reel to post. She does not want to hunt through apps. Home puts the day on one screen.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What sits at the top of Home, and in what order.
- How Riya sets up water and her own reminders.
- How Breathe and focus, the daily brief and Getting started work.
- How to read xp and Up next, and what an admin can change.

## Step one: the header

Home opens with a dark card. It shows the date, "Hi, Riya!" and three quick links: her level, her streak and today''s mood. Beside them:

- **My page:** opens her public page.
- **The bell:** opens her Inbox, with a count of what is unread.
- **Her picture:** opens the Profile tab.
- **Search anything:** a search box. Results show right under it as she types: apps, help, her tasks, her journal, people and more.

## Step two: water and reminders

Next is **Water and reminders**. Riya taps **+ Glass** each time she drinks. The card opens into three tabs.

1. **Water:** a glass that fills up, the count against her goal, and the last seven days.
2. **Settings:** her **Daily goal**, the size of **A glass**, **How often** (every two or four hours) and the hours, **From** and **To**. **Remind me to drink** sends notes to her Inbox until the goal is met.
3. **Reminders:** her own reminders. She taps **+ Add a reminder**, types "Back up the drive" under **Remind me to**, sets a time and picks the **Days**. Each one lands in her Inbox on time.

The Reminders tab also lists her tasks tagged Remember.

> **Pro tip:** a water note never stacks up. One line a day is rewritten with the new count, and it is cleared once the day is over.

## Step three: the line of the day

Under it sits one short line for the day. It takes turns: a line from a philosopher, then a line written for the app. Every line is shown once before any comes back. It costs nothing to set up. It is just there.

## Step four: Breathe and focus

Next is **Breathe and focus**, the same panel as in Mindfulness. It has four tabs.

1. **Breathe:** breathe with circles that fill. Calm exhale is the first pattern. A finished breath logs mindful minutes.
2. **Timer:** one countdown, no breaks.
3. **Pomodoro:** focus parts with breaks. Presets like Classic 25/5 live in the settings sheet.
4. **Stopwatch:** counts up from zero.

Riya picks Pomodoro, sets 50 minutes on the ruler and taps **Start**. The clock keeps going while she moves around the app. A small pill in the corner shows the time left.

## Step five: the daily brief and Getting started

**Your daily brief** shows three things that changed and three next steps, from her own numbers. The steps never point at an app she has off. It runs on set steps, so it never spends credits.

Under it, a new account sees **Getting started**. It lists seven first steps:

- Check in on Daily.
- Tick your first task.
- Do a small act.
- Pick two interests.
- Choose your username.
- Join a circle, an event or a run club.
- Take the strategy session.

Each one ticks only when Riya does the thing. It pays nothing. She can fold it into a small toggle, and it goes by itself after fourteen days.

## Step six: the numbers

Below that, Home shows her numbers before any actions:

- **Your metrics:** her Life score, mood and wellbeing. She taps one for the detail.
- **Daily:** today''s boxes, to tick right here.
- **Up next:** events, runs and circles she joined. With nothing booked, it says where to find them.
- **Xp, last 14 days:** **By day** shows a line, with a green dot for a day she ticked a task. **By source** shows a ring of what the xp came from.
- **Your pillars:** a bar for each of the seven.
- **Go to:** every app she has switched on.

Some parts show only when their app is on, like Life, Knowledge and Money.

> **Result:** in one scroll, Riya drinks a glass, starts a 50-minute focus part, reads her brief and ticks her check-in. She never left Home.

## What an admin checks

Home is core and always on. What shows on it follows the apps that are on.

1. **Flags:** in **Admin**, the **Flags** tab switches an app for everyone, for a share of people, or for one person. If the team turns Mindfulness off for everyone, Breathe and focus leaves Home.
2. **One person:** in **People**, tap Riya''s row, then use **What does this person see?** to allow or block an app for her alone.
3. **For the team:** staff see an extra block in their own brief, **For the team**, about work waiting for them.
4. **Up next:** it fills from Party, Kreators Run and Circles. If those are off, there is nothing to book.
5. **Water and reminders:** these notes are sent by the server''s timer. Every timer run that did something shows under **Logs**, which only a super admin reads.
6. **The line of the day:** it lives in the app''s code. It cannot be changed from Admin.

> **Pro tip for admins:** before you switch an app off for everyone, open **Observability**. It shows who is here and which apps are on.

## The bottom line

Home is the day in one place: water, a line, a focus clock, a brief and the numbers. Riya sets it up once and it keeps up with her. As an admin, you shape Home through the apps you switch on and off.', 5, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l05', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Profile, apps and the public page', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya wants two things now. She wants the app to show only what she uses. And she wants brands to find her work in one link. The Profile tab does the first. Her public page does the second.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What sits on the Profile tab, from top to bottom.
- How Riya switches apps, sets how groups open and puts apps in her own order.
- How she builds her public page, with links and switches.
- Where her badges and her year live, and what an admin can do.

## Step one: the Profile tab

The Profile tab opens with a cover: her picture, her name and a small badge for her role. **Sign out** sits at the top right and always asks first. Three buttons sit on the cover: **See my public page**, **Edit page** and **Plans and credits**.

## Step two: who you are

Under **Who you are** are the tags the setup chat gave her, like Content creator and Freelancer.

1. Riya taps **Change my tags and apps**.
2. The setup chat opens again, with her last answers filled in.
3. She now shoots for brands too, so under For clients she adds **Shooting**. Then she switches on the apps it suggests.

Tags pick her apps and fill in her skills and interests. They never show on her public page.

## Step three: Appearance and Features

**Appearance** has three buttons: **Match device**, **Light** and **Dark**.

**Features** lists her apps in groups, always in this order: Contribution, Reflection, Connection, Creation and Essentials. A sixth group, Operations, is for the team only.

1. Each group shows how many apps are on, like "3 of 7 on". Riya opens Creation.
2. Each app has a switch. Off hides an app; nothing is deleted.
3. An app that fits her answers says **Fits you**, and why.
4. Each group has a switch, **Starts open on Apps and in the menu**. She turns it on for Contribution and Creation, the two she uses most.
5. She taps **Arrange apps**. Now she can drag an app by its handle, or use **Move up** and **Move down**. Apps stay inside their group. She taps **Done**.
6. To undo an order, the group''s More menu has **Reset order**.

> **Pro tip:** the order and the open groups are saved on her account, so they follow her to every device. The Apps page and the menu use them too.

## Step four: privacy, account and data

- **Privacy choices:** the same three choices as the first privacy card. Each tick saves at once.
- **Account:** her email, **Change password** and **Sign out**.
- **Your data:** **Export my data** gives everything as one file. **Erase everything** clears her pillars, xp, streak and badges, and asks first. **Delete my account** asks her to type DELETE.

## Step five: points, her year and badges

1. **Points** shows her balance, with **Rewards** to spend them and **Rank** for the leaderboard.
2. **Your year** is a grid of days, like a calendar for the whole year. It shows xp or tasks ticked each day. Only she sees it.
3. **Badges** are medals the app earns for her by its own count. She taps one to see the next step. Some families, like Focus and Money, show only to her.
4. **Awarded** lists honors the team gave her, or that time in the app unlocked.

Lower down, the profile form has **Show me on the leaderboard**. She ticks it and taps **Save profile**.

## Step six: the public page

Riya taps **Edit page**. This is the Public page app.

1. **Username:** her page address, like /u/riya_edits. Three to twenty letters, numbers or underscores.
2. **Bio**, **Photo link** and **Links** for Instagram, YouTube and a website.
3. **Time zone**, **Currency** and **City**.
4. **What is public:** ten switches. **My page is public** comes first, and every other switch needs it on. The rest are social icons, links, portfolio, what she sells, posts, activity, badges, friend requests and chats.
5. She taps **Save profile**. At the top, **Copy** copies her page link and **Open** shows it.

Then she builds the **Links** part, like a link-in-bio page.

1. She adds a block: **Link**, **Header**, **Text**, **Image**, **Video** or **Carousel**.
2. For her showreel she picks **Link**, adds the button text and the https address, and ticks **Featured: big and in colour**.
3. She taps **Add to my page**. Each change saves on its own.
4. **Hide** takes a block off the page without deleting it.
5. Each block shows how many taps it got, for her eyes only.

Her journal and wellbeing never appear on her page. The page ends with "Made with kreators.app".

> **Result:** Riya sees only her apps, in her order. Brands get one link with her showreel on top, and she can see which links they tap.

## What an admin checks

1. **One person''s apps:** in **Admin**, **People**, tap her row. Under **What does this person see?** tick to allow and untick to block. A struck-through app is not allowed for her. **Reset** brings the usual rules back. Locked apps, like Daily, cannot be blocked.
2. **Everyone''s apps:** the **Flags** tab sets an app''s on or off, its **Minimum role** and its **Rollout %**. An override can force it for one person, one role or one brand, and can expire.
3. **Badges and honors:** in her row, **Badges given by hand** and **Honors**. A badge she earned is never taken away there.
4. **Her page:** you cannot change her page. If a page breaks the terms, the account can be deleted from People, after a sheet asks first.
5. **Her tags:** tags are private. They never reach her public page.

## The bottom line

Profile is Riya''s control room: tags, apps, privacy, data and badges. The public page is her front door, built from blocks and guarded by switches she owns. As an admin, you change what apps people can open, and leave their page and tags to them.', 6, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l06', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Journal and prompts', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya edits videos all day. By night her head is full of client notes, cuts and half ideas. The Journal is where she puts it down, and nobody else ever reads it. Not a brand. Not a coach. Not you, the admin.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya opens the Journal and writes her first entry.
- How the calendar lets her add or fix any past day.
- How Prompt me gives her a question when she does not know what to write.
- What an admin can and cannot do with the Journal.

## Where the Journal lives

The Journal is in the Reflection group of apps. It is on for every new account, so Riya does not need to switch it on. She finds it under Apps, in the Reflection group, or she taps Journal from the Wellbeing screen.

The screen opens with a back link to Wellbeing and the title Journal. Under it sits the water and reminders section. On a laptop it is a small bubble at the left instead.

## Step by step: Riya''s first entry

1. Riya opens the Journal and taps **New entry**. It is the one solid button on the screen.
2. In **Title** she types "Long edit day".
3. In **Say anything that is on your mind** she writes a few lines. A count of her words shows beside the label.
4. Under **Emotion** she taps a face: Low, Sad, Neutral, Happy or Overjoyed. She can tap it again to clear it.
5. She moves the **Stress level** slider from 1 to 5.
6. Under **Stressor** she taps Work. She can pick more than one, from Work, Money, Family, Health, Loneliness, Relationship, Sleep and Something else.
7. She taps **Save entry**.

The entry now shows under **My journals**, with its face, the time, the stressors she picked and the first lines of what she wrote.

## The hero and the calendar

Once Riya has an entry, a sky blue card shows at the top. It says how many entries she has so far and how many this month. Two small pills count the positive entries and the heavy ones.

Inside the card is **This month**, a calendar. Every day is a button.

1. Riya taps last Tuesday. A panel opens right under the calendar, never in a popup.
2. It lists what she wrote that day. She taps one to open it.
3. If the day is empty, she taps **Write an entry for this day**. If it has entries, the button reads **Add another entry for this day**.
4. She closes the day with the round close button, or with Escape.

A day in the future cannot take an entry. The server refuses it.

## Prompt me

Some nights Riya stares at the box. Under New entry is a section called **Not sure what to write?**

1. She picks a **Category** from the dropdown, or leaves it on Every category.
2. She taps **Prompt me**.
3. A card shows the prompt, its category, and about how many minutes it takes.
4. She opens **Why this prompt** to see why it helps and how to write it.
5. She opens **What to expect** to see what may come up.
6. She taps **Write about this**. The entry sheet opens with the prompt as its title and first line.
7. If it does not fit, she taps **Another prompt**.

There are sixteen categories, such as Shadow work, Inner child, Gratitude and joy, Energy and boundaries, Money and abundance mindset, 3-6-9 writing and Weekly reset. A line under the card says how many she has seen. None comes back until she has seen them all.

Which prompts she has seen stays on her own device. It is never sent to the server. The heavy categories end with a care note: stop, and talk to a professional or someone you trust.

## Changing or deleting an entry

1. Riya taps an entry in My journals. The sheet opens with her words.
2. She changes what she likes and taps **Save changes**.
3. To remove it, she taps **Delete entry**.
4. A box asks "Delete this entry?" and says the words are gone for good.
5. She taps the red **Yes, delete it**, or **Keep it** to back out.

## The rest of the screen

- **By period:** a block that picks a week, month, quarter or half year and shows her journal and wellbeing numbers for it.
- **Emerging:** patterns from her own logs, when there is one ready. It reads when she wrote, never what she wrote.
- **How it works:** the steps of the app, folded at the foot.

> **Pro tip:** tell new people to pick an emotion on every entry. The Life app''s emotional area and the Journal''s hero both read it. An entry with no emotion still counts as written, but says less.

> **Result:** Riya has a private page for her thoughts, a calendar to fill any past day, and a prompt whenever she is stuck.

## What the admin checks

The Journal is the most private part of the app. You cannot read it, and you should never try to.

- **No staff view:** no admin screen shows a journal entry. Journal data never reaches brands, staff, the leaderboard or Club picks.
- **Coaches never see it:** a life coach can see many things a student shares, but never the journal.
- **The assistant:** it reads the journal only for its owner, only when the owner asks about it, and only through a model that keeps no data.
- **Logs stay clean:** the Logs page never holds journal words. At most it keeps a bare error line with no person and no path.
- **Switching it:** in Admin, **Flags**, you can turn the Journal off for everyone, set a rollout share or a minimum role. Use this with care, since it is on for all new accounts.
- **One person:** in Admin, **People**, open a person and use **What does this person see?** to tick or untick Journal for them.
- **Their data, their call:** a person''s export holds their entries, and deleting the account deletes them.

If a member writes to Help about a lost entry, you cannot restore it. A deleted entry is gone for good, and the app says so before it goes.

## The bottom line

The Journal is Riya''s alone. Your job as an admin is to keep it switched on and to keep it private. Help people find Prompt me and the calendar, and never ask them to share what they wrote.', 7, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l07', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Mood, Sleep, Stress, Wellbeing and Life', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya can tell when a deadline week hits her. She sleeps late, her mood dips and her stress climbs. Five small apps turn that feeling into numbers she can see, and one dashboard ties them to the five areas of life.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya switches on the trackers and logs her first day.
- How Wellbeing turns four trackers and a streak into one score.
- How Life scores the five areas of life, in their fixed order.
- What an admin can see and switch for these apps.

## Switch them on

Life is on for every new account. Wellbeing, Mood, Sleep and Stress start off, unless the setup chat switched one on for her.

1. Riya opens **Apps** and taps the **Reflection** group. It says how many of its apps are on.
2. She turns on the switch beside **Wellbeing**, **Mood**, **Sleep** and **Stress**.
3. She can do the same under the **Profile** tab, in Features.

## Mood: one face a day

1. Riya opens **Mood**. The top asks "How are you feeling today?"
2. She taps a face: Low, Sad, Neutral, Happy or Overjoyed.
3. She taps **Set mood**. A card asks why she feels that way.
4. She moves three sliders: **How active were you?**, **How well did you eat?** and **How was your sleep?**
5. She can type a short note in **Anything else**. It is optional.
6. She taps **Save today''s mood**.

Below sit **This week**, **Last 30 days** and **This month**. The month is a calendar. She taps a past day to add, change or remove its mood. A future day is refused.

## Sleep: last night in hours

1. Riya opens **Sleep** and finds **Last night**.
2. She drags the ruler to the hours she slept, in half hours.
3. Under **Quality** she taps Rough, Light, Okay, Good or Deep.
4. She taps **Save last night**. If she already logged it, the button reads **Update last night**.

The hero shows her average this week against eight hours. Seven to eight hours scores full marks. Under **Nights** she can delete a night, and a sheet asks first.

## Stress: the level and what is behind it

1. Riya opens **Stress** and finds **Right now**.
2. She moves the slider. The word above it changes: Calm, Fine, Tense, Stressed or Overwhelmed.
3. Under **What is behind it** she taps Work and Money.
4. She taps **Save today**.

The bubbles in **What is behind it** show which stressor came up most in thirty days. Lower is calmer. A line that climbs for a week is worth a word with someone.

## Wellbeing: one score

Wellbeing pulls the four trackers together.

- **The score:** a number out of 100, scored over the last seven days.
- **What makes it:** a ring shows the parts. Mood counts most, then sleep, then stress, then mindful minutes and the streak.
- **The rows:** Mood, Sleep, Stress and Mindfulness, each with today''s value and a small line. Riya taps one to log.
- **Two doors:** Journal to write it down, and Companion to talk it through.

A part with nothing logged sits in the middle. It does not drag her down.

## Life: the five areas

Life scores the five areas of life, always in this order: physical, financial, mental, emotional, spiritual. Each one holds up the next. When one fails, it pulls down the one after it.

1. Riya opens **Life**. The hero shows her Life score, the average of the five areas.
2. **The five areas, this week** shows a bar for each, and which went up or down.
3. **This week, day by day** shows sleep, calm and mood as scores out of a hundred.
4. Each area has its own card with a ring, its parts and a link to its tools.

The parts come from what she already logs:

- **Physical:** Sleep, Movement and Breath.
- **Financial:** This month, Budgets, Invoices and Goals, from Finance.
- **Mental:** Stress, Focus from ticked tasks, and Learning from School.
- **Emotional:** Mood, Journal and People pillars.
- **Spiritual:** Stillness, Small acts, and Teachers and faith.

Each part is a link to the place she acts on it. A part with no data says "no data" and does not count against her.

## Log movement

Movement only lives in Life.

1. Riya scrolls to **Log movement**.
2. She types 40 in the minutes box for her evening walk.
3. She taps **Add minutes**.

A hundred and fifty minutes a week gives full marks for movement.

> **Pro tip:** tell new people to log for one full week before they judge a score. Seven days of mood, sleep and stress make the Wellbeing ring and the Life bars honest.

> **Result:** Riya sees in one place that her deadline weeks cost her sleep and calm, and she can tap straight to the fix.

## What the admin checks

Wellbeing numbers are private, like the journal.

- **No staff view:** mood, sleep, stress and the Wellbeing score never reach brands, staff, the leaderboard or Club picks. There is no admin screen for them.
- **Coaches:** a life coach sees these numbers only if the student leaves the Reflection switch on for that coach. Never a tracker''s note.
- **The assistant:** tracker reads are offered only through a model that keeps no data.
- **Switches:** in Admin, **Flags**, each of the five apps has its own row: On, Minimum role, Rollout % and Overrides.
- **One person:** in Admin, **People**, use **What does this person see?** to tick or untick an app for one person.
- **No pay:** logging these trackers pays no xp, so there is nothing to take back or check for cheating.

If someone says a score looks wrong, ask what they logged this week. A score is built only from their own logs.

## The bottom line

Mood, Sleep and Stress take a minute a day. Wellbeing and Life turn those minutes into a picture. As an admin, keep the apps switched on for the people who need them, and keep their numbers out of every staff view.', 8, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l08', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Mindfulness: breathe and focus', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya has a forty-minute edit to finish before lunch. Her phone buzzes and her mind wanders. Mindfulness gives her one calm place to breathe for two minutes, then focus on that one task with a clock that keeps going even when she leaves the page.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya breathes with the circles and logs mindful minutes.
- How she runs a Timer, a Pomodoro and a Stopwatch on a task.
- How she sets sounds, a daily goal and a nudge.
- What an admin can switch and check for this app.

## Switch it on

Mindfulness starts off on a new account. Riya opens **Apps**, taps the **Reflection** group and turns on the switch beside **Mindfulness**. The screen opens from Wellbeing too.

The hero shows her mindful minutes this week. Under it is one dark card called **Breathe and focus**, with four tabs: **Breathe**, **Timer**, **Pomodoro** and **Stopwatch**.

## Step by step: Breathe

1. Riya taps the **Breathe** tab.
2. She picks a pattern. Calm exhale is first: in for 4, hold for 4, out for 6, rest for 2. Others are Box, 4-7-8, Even, Triangle, Longer out and Sigh.
3. She picks a cue: Soft chime, Bell, Voice or No cue.
4. She taps a quick length, such as 3 minutes, or drags the ruler from 1 to 30.
5. She taps **Start**. A row of glass circles fills one step at a time, each with the word and the seconds left.

A finished breath always logs mindful minutes. If a count feels too fast, she taps the "per count" button beside the total and drags the ruler to hold each count longer.

## Step by step: Timer on a task

1. Riya taps the round **Focus on a task** button at the top right of the section.
2. The sheet shows her recent tasks and a search. She picks "Cut the brand reel". She could pick **No task** instead.
3. She taps the **Timer** tab and sets 40 minutes on the ruler.
4. She taps **Start**. The tab title now shows the time and her task.

If she taps Stop past a whole minute, a sheet asks **Stop here?** She picks **Keep 23 minutes** or **Throw it away**. Under a minute, nothing is kept.

On Daily, a task with a time estimate shows **Start focus** with its minutes. That opens Mindfulness with the task picked and the first round set to that length.

## Pomodoro and Stopwatch

- **Pomodoro:** focus parts with short and long breaks. Riya picks Short break, Long break, Sessions between long breaks and Total sessions from four small chips. **Skip** moves to the next part. **Stop** goes back to session one.
- **This part:** while a part runs, plus or minus 1 and 5 nudge its length.
- **Stopwatch:** counts up. Stop keeps the whole minutes, if that switch is on. Reset keeps nothing.
- **Rate it:** after a focus session she can tap how focused she was, from Scattered to Deep, or Skip. It pays nothing and only she sees it.

## The clock follows her

When Riya leaves Mindfulness, the clock keeps running. A small pill shows the time in a corner. Its colour moves from blue to green to orange to red as time runs out, and turns violet in a break. On a laptop she can pause, restart or stop from it.

If she opens the same clock in two tabs, only one tab logs it. The other shows "Running in another tab".

## Settings and sounds

1. Riya taps the round **Timer settings** button.
2. Under **Your plan** she picks Classic 25/5, Deep 50/10 or Light 15/3.
3. She checks three switches. Finished focus sessions count as mindful minutes by default. Breaks do not. Starting the next part by itself is off.
4. She turns on **A daily focus goal** and sets 90 minutes. It shows under the timer as "50 of 90 minutes today". There is no streak to lose.
5. She turns on **Remind me to focus**, picks a time and the days. One inbox note comes on those days, and none on a day she already focused.

The round sound button is the master switch for every sound. The **Background music** button opens Rain, Ocean, Wind, Soft hum or Lo-fi, with a volume. All of them are made in the browser. **Full screen** makes the section cover the page.

These settings are saved on her account, so they follow her to her laptop.

## Today, the dashboard and Sessions

- **Today:** under the panel, today''s sessions, newest first. Each has **Continue** to start the same clock again. She taps a row to change its length or task, or to delete it.
- **Add a session I forgot:** adds one for today or yesterday.
- **How productive you were:** focus time and tasks ticked, by Days, Weeks or Months, in Minutes or Hours.
- **Sessions:** her mindful logs. Each has a trash button, and a sheet asks "Delete this session?" with a red **Yes, delete it** and **Keep it**.

> **Pro tip:** on a laptop, Space starts or pauses, S stops and K skips in Pomodoro. Press ? to see the list. Keys never fire while she types in a box.

> **Result:** Riya breathes for three minutes, focuses for forty on one task, and sees her focus time add up across the week.

## What the admin checks

- **No pay to police:** logging mindful minutes pays no xp. Deleting a session takes nothing back.
- **Private numbers:** mindful minutes are wellbeing data. They reach a coach only through the student''s Reflection switch, never a brand or staff screen.
- **Switches:** in Admin, **Flags**, the Mindfulness row sets On, Minimum role, Rollout % and Overrides. In **People**, **What does this person see?** sets it for one person.
- **Sound help:** if someone hears nothing, ask them to tap Start once. Browsers only allow sound after a tap. Then check the master sound switch.
- **Fullscreen help:** Escape, the same button, or leaving the page ends full screen.

## The bottom line

Mindfulness is one card with four clocks. Teach people to pick a task first, then pick a clock. As an admin, keep it switched on, and point people to the settings sheet before they ask for a feature that is already there.', 9, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l09', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Reviews: looking back by period', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya worked hard last month. But did she move the things that matter, or only the urgent ones? A review answers that. The app brings the numbers from every app she uses. She brings the words.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya picks a period and writes her first review.
- How templates give each kind of review its own questions.
- How the review calendar reminds her on the day.
- How a task is brought to a review, and how business metrics work.
- What an admin can check.

## Switch it on

Reviews start off on a new account. Riya opens **Apps**, taps the **Reflection** group and turns on the switch beside **Reviews**.

## The four periods

A review covers one period:

- **Week:** seven days, Monday to Sunday. The built-in one is the Weekly review.
- **Month:** a calendar month, the Monthly review.
- **Quarter:** three months, the Quarterly review.
- **Half year:** six months, the Mid-year check-in.

Each period has one review per person. A period that has not started yet cannot be reviewed.

## Step by step: Riya''s first monthly review

1. Riya opens **Reviews** and uses the period picker at the top to choose Month and last month.
2. The hero shows the period, with her xp, tasks done and check-ins.
3. Under **The numbers**, each tile sits next to the month before. She taps a tile to go where it comes from.
4. **Day by day** shows her xp per day, and a ring of xp by pillar.
5. Under **Your review**, she answers the questions. Short answers are fine.
6. Under **How did it go?** she taps a face.
7. She taps **Save this review**.

The review is saved with the numbers as they were. New data or a changed template never rewrites it. She can change her words and save again. Her saved reviews show under **Past reviews**.

## Templates

The built-in template for each period stays, and cannot be edited or removed. Riya can add her own.

1. Under **Templates**, she taps **New template**.
2. In **Name** she types "Money month".
3. In **Comes back every** she picks Month.
4. In **Questions, one per line** she writes "What did I earn?" and "What did I waste?"
5. She taps **Add template**.

Next month, a **Questions from** box lets her choose between the built-in questions and hers. She can keep up to twelve templates.

## The review calendar

1. Under **Review calendar**, Riya taps **Schedule a review**, or taps a day on the calendar.
2. Under **Which review** she picks Month.
3. She sets **First day** and **Time**, and an optional **Name**.
4. Under **Repeat** she picks Every month. It can also be Every day, Weekdays, Every week on chosen days or Every year, with an end day.
5. She taps **Schedule it**.

On that day, at that time, one note lands in her inbox. On the first day of a period, it opens the one that just ended. A bell on the calendar is a reminder. A tick is a review she wrote. Each scheduled review has a **Remind me** switch, and removing one asks first in a sheet. Reviews she wrote stay. She can schedule up to twenty.

## Bring a task to the review

Riya has a task, "Raise my day rate", that she wants to think about at month end.

1. On Daily, she opens the task''s plan.
2. She turns on **Bring it to my review**.
3. In that month''s review, it shows under **Tasks you brought**, done or still open.
4. She taps **Add to my answer**. It adds a line to her answer and never overwrites it.

A repeating task carries the flag to the next one.

## Business metrics

Riya runs her freelance work like a small studio, so she uses this.

1. Below The numbers on Reviews, under **Business metrics**, she turns on **Show business metrics**. It is off until she does.
2. She taps **Add a metric**.
3. In **Where does the number come from**, she picks **I type it in**, or a number read from Agency, such as Pieces published, Approved first time, Published on time, Billable hours, Utilisation, Invoiced, Paid or New clients.
4. She names it, sets a target and says whether more or less is better.
5. She taps **Add this metric**.

Each metric then sits against its target for the period on screen. She can keep up to twelve.

> **Pro tip:** schedule the weekly review for Sunday evening and the monthly one for the first of the month. The note opens the right period, so it takes one tap to start.

> **Result:** Riya has a review for every month, the same questions each time, a reminder on the day, and the tasks she flagged waiting for her.

## What the admin checks

- **Private words:** a review is the owner''s. Journal and wellbeing numbers in it go only to its owner.
- **Coaches:** a coach sees saved reviews only while the student''s Reflection switch is on, and never the snapshot of numbers.
- **Business metrics:** the switch is the person''s own. While it is off, the server refuses everything but the switch. The Agency numbers come from that person''s own Agency records.
- **Reminders:** the cron sends each reminder once. A day missed while nothing ran is skipped, never sent late.
- **Switches:** in Admin, **Flags**, the Reviews row sets On, Minimum role, Rollout % and Overrides. In **People**, **What does this person see?** sets it for one person.

If someone says a reminder did not come, check that the scheduled review''s **Remind me** switch is on and its time is in their own zone.

## The bottom line

Reviews turn a busy month into a clear look back. Teach people to pick a period, answer a few questions and schedule the next one. As an admin, keep Reviews switched on for those who want them, and remember the words are theirs alone.', 10, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l10', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Discover: the quiz and its add-ons', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya knows she is good at editing. She is less sure why some weeks she finishes everything and other weeks she starts five things and ends none. Discover is a quiz on how she thinks, feels and acts. It shows her strengths across the seven relationships, and it never compares her with anyone else.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya takes the main quiz, part by part.
- How she reads her result and decides what it may shape.
- How the add-ons work, from 16 personality types to Human design and numerology.
- What an admin can see, and what stays with Riya.

## Switch it on

Discover starts off on a new account. Riya can switch it on in two ways:

- **From Apps:** open the **Reflection** group and turn on the switch beside **Discover**.
- **From the setup chat:** its last step offers **Take the Discover quiz**, which switches the app on first.

## Step by step: the main quiz

1. Riya opens **Discover**. A sky blue card says "Find out what you are good at".
2. She taps **Start the quiz**. The quiz has five short parts and saves as she goes, so she can stop and come back.
3. **Priorities and preferences:** three statements at a time. She picks the one most like her and the one least like her.
4. **Puzzles, part 1:** pictures to solve. They get harder when she does well and easier when she does not. There is no time limit.
5. **You, in words:** ten words at a time. She picks three most like her and three least like her.
6. **Puzzles, part 2:** more puzzles that keep adjusting to her.
7. **Real situations:** she picks the most and the least effective way to handle each case.
8. She moves with **Back** and **Next**.

After the five parts, a card says "Your strengths are ready". She can tap **Add emotional intelligence**, tap **See my strengths now**, or tap **Go back**.

The emotional intelligence add-on has three more parts: Your feelings, Reading people and With people. It counts only when all of it is answered.

## Reading the result

The result opens with tabs:

- **Strengths:** her top three of seven, such as Getting it done, Connecting or Learning, plus her quietest strength and a link to its pillar.
- **Personality:** her five traits (Curiosity, Follow-through, Energy with people, Warmth and Calm) with what each means for her.
- **Reasoning:** the level her puzzles settled at. It says plainly that this is not an IQ score.
- **Emotional intelligence:** only if she took it.
- **16 types, love, mind:** the add-ons.
- **Use my results:** her switches.

At the foot she can tap **Take it again** or **Delete this result**. Delete asks first and is final.

## Use my results

1. Riya opens **Use my results**, under **Let your results help**.
2. **Shape my next steps** is on to start. Home''s daily brief then picks steps that fit her. It is plain rules, no AI.
3. **Let the assistant learn from them** is off until she turns it on. Only a model that keeps no data can read the short summary.
4. **Include how my mind works** is off until she turns it on. It never names a condition.

## The add-ons

Under **More ways to know yourself**, four self-checks sit as cards. Each says Not taken yet, how far she got, or when she took it. Its button reads **Start**, **Carry on** or **See my result**.

- **16 personality types:** a free check in the style of the Myers-Briggs test, not the official one. Twenty-four lines give a four-letter type.
- **Love languages:** twenty choices between two lines. Ten on how she likes to be shown love, ten on how she shows it. The gap is said plainly.
- **How your mind works:** twenty-four lines on attention, energy and impulse, and patterns and senses. It is never a diagnosis, and says so.
- **Human design:** a form, explained below.

## Step by step: Human design and numerology

1. Riya opens the **Human design** card.
2. She fills **Birth date**, **Birth time** and the **Time zone of the place you were born**, such as Kolkata.
3. For numerology she can add her **Full name on your birth record**. It is optional.
4. She taps **Work out my chart**.
5. She picks the zodiac: **Tropical** or **Sidereal (Fagan-Bradley)**. The sidereal reading is marked as an experiment.
6. She picks a layer: **3D · Body and day**, **5D · Mind and people**, **7D · Purpose** or **Numerology**.
7. Under **Goals you can do and count**, she taps **Add as a task**. It lands under that pillar''s project, in Not scheduled yet on Daily.

Human design and numerology are shown as systems for reflection, not science. They never predict.

> **Pro tip:** tell people to take the main quiz first. Numerology then compares its numbers with their own Discover answers, instead of only offering an invitation.

> **Result:** Riya knows her top strengths, why she starts many things, and has one small task from her chart waiting on Daily.

## What the admin checks

- **Owner only:** Discover answers and results are like the journal. They are never shown to brands, staff, the leaderboard or a coach.
- **The assistant:** it sees only a short profile, and only while the person''s switch is on.
- **Birth details:** they stay on the person''s own table. Only type, strategy, authority and profile can reach the assistant or next steps.
- **Observability:** it counts each add-on finished and how many switched how your mind works on. Counts only, no names or answers.
- **Switches:** in Admin, **Flags**, the Discover row sets On, Minimum role, Rollout % and Overrides. In **People**, **What does this person see?** sets it for one person.
- **Data:** the answers are in the person''s export and go with erase and delete.

If someone asks whether their score is good or bad, the answer is that Discover has no pass mark. It only shows what they lean on most.

## The bottom line

Discover helps people see their own strengths in plain words. Teach them to finish the five parts, then decide what the result may shape. As an admin, keep it switched on, and never ask anyone to share what it found.', 11, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l11', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Companion, Coaching and Emerging', 'lesson', NULL, 'Some days Riya wants to talk a day through. Some months she wants a real person to keep her on track. And sometimes she wants to know what her own logs are telling her. Three parts of the app cover this: the Companion, Coaching and Emerging.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya chats with the Companion, and when it uses credits.
- How she finds a life coach and picks what the coach sees.
- How Emerging shows her patterns, including good and bad habits.
- What an admin does for each.

## The Companion

The Companion is a chat that knows how her day is going. It is not a therapist, and not for emergencies. It starts off, so Riya turns it on under **Apps**, in the **Reflection** group.

1. Riya opens **Companion**. The hero shows her check-in streak.
2. With no lines yet, she can tap a starter, such as "I feel tired today" or "I am stressed about work".
3. Or she types in **Say what is on your mind** and sends it.
4. While it answers, she can tap the stop square.
5. To clear the chat, she taps the trash button. A sheet asks "Clear this conversation?" and says it cannot be undone.

Each reply says who answered. With a plan, credits and room in the day''s cap, a model answers and she can pick it. Without a plan, or when credits or the day''s cap run out, the built-in companion answers for free. It never shows a paywall.

If her words show she may be in danger, it tells her to call the local emergency number, go to the nearest hospital, or call Tele-MANAS on 14416 in India.

## Coaching: finding a life coach

Coaching starts off too. Riya turns it on in the **Reflection** group.

1. Riya opens **Coaching** and scrolls to **Find a life coach**. It lists coaches the team chose who are taking new students.
2. She taps **Ask to be coached** on one. The coach says yes or no.
3. Once the coach says yes, her coach shows under **Your life coach**, here and on her **Profile** tab.
4. Under **What they see**, she picks from five switches: Daily and streaks, Pillars and pyramids, Routine and contract, Life map answers, and Reflection. All five start on.
5. She can tap **End coaching** at any time. A sheet asks first.

A coach can also ask her, by email, @username or friend code. She then taps **Say yes** or **Say no**. She can have up to three coaches. After a no, the same coach can ask again in thirty days.

A coach never sees her journal, a tracker''s note, her money, the Companion or a review''s snapshot. With Pillars and pyramids on, a coach can add and edit tasks under her projects. Those tasks wear the tag From your coach. A coach never ticks or removes them.

**Notes from your coach** lists every note, and each one lands in her inbox. The **Change log** shows every ask, yes, note and task change, with the time.

## Becoming a coach

If Riya coaches other editors, she can ask under **Are you a life coach?** She writes who she coaches and taps **Ask the team**. She can also say "I''m a life coach" in the setup chat.

## Emerging: patterns in her own logs

Emerging reads across the apps Riya uses and finds things that happen together. It never says one thing causes another. There is nothing to switch on.

1. On Home, under **Emerging**, Riya sees her top patterns. She taps **See all**.
2. The **Emerging** page shows two counts, Patterns found and Still watching.
3. **What stands out** lists patterns, strongest first.
4. **Good and bad habits** compares the days she ticked a task with the days she did not, using mood, sleep, stress, tasks and focus.
5. **Still watching** says what each pattern still needs.

Each card has a number, a tag, one sentence, a small chart and one action:

- **Good habit:** her "Evening walk" goes with better mood. She taps **Make it a habit**, checks the repeat, the first day and the time, then taps **Keep**.
- **Bad habit:** a habit that goes with worse days. She taps **Add to not to do** to put it on her not to do list.
- **More:** **Not now** hides a card on this device.

On Daily, a **Habits** section sits under Not to do with her good and bad habits.

> **Pro tip:** a habit needs a task ticked on at least three days in eight weeks, and at least five days with it and five without, before it can be called good or bad. Tell people to keep logging mood and sleep. Emerging can only compare what they log.

> **Result:** Riya talks her day through, has a coach who sees only what she allows, and knows which habits lift her days.

## What the admin checks

- **Companion plans:** in Admin, **People**, the Plan column starts or ends a plan, and the Credits column shows what is left. Plans are only started by an admin until payments go live.
- **Life coaches:** in Admin, **Life coaches**, the table **Asked to be a life coach** lists asks. Tick rows and tap **Make life coach**, or **Say no**. The person is told.
- **Taking the badge:** in the **Life coaches** table, **Take the badge** ends every link that coach has, and their students are told. It asks first.
- **Linking:** under **Link a coach and a student**, pick a coach, enter the student and choose **Ask the student** or **Link now**. Use Link now only if the student already agreed.
- **Emerging:** it is computed when read and stored nowhere. No staff screen shows it, and it never reaches a coach, the assistant or the leaderboard.
- **Switches:** in Admin, **Flags**, the Companion and Coaching rows set On, Minimum role, Rollout % and Overrides.

## The bottom line

The Companion listens, a coach guides, and Emerging shows what Riya''s days already say. As an admin, choose coaches with care, keep plans and credits honest, and leave the patterns to the person they belong to.', 12, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l12', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Community and channels: posts, reactions and guides', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya edits videos all day, mostly alone. She wants a place to ask a question, share a win and help someone newer. Community is that place, and guides keep it kind.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya writes her first post and earns from it.
- How reactions, replies, mentions and follows work.
- How anonymous posts stay anonymous.
- How channels work beside the feed.
- How a guide holds, pins, hides and mutes, and how you make one.

## Switch it on

Community is off on a new account. Riya opens **Apps**, taps the **Connection** group and turns on the switch beside **Community**.

## Step by step: Riya''s first post

1. Riya opens **Community**. It opens on **Feed**, with **Channels** beside it.
2. She taps **Write a post**.
3. Under **Pick a category** she taps **Creative**. The others are Self care, Mindfulness, Stories, Support, Learning, Stress, Affirmation and Awareness.
4. Under **Your post** she writes: "Finished my first brand edit in Pune this week. What do you charge for a reel?"
5. She leaves **Post anonymously** off, so her name shows.
6. She taps **Create post**.

## What a post pays

- **A post:** 5 xp, up to three a day.
- **A reply:** 2 xp, up to five a day.
- **A reaction you give:** 1 xp, up to ten a day.
- **A reaction on your post:** a point for you.
- **A new follower:** two points, up to twenty points a day from both.

Each thing pays once. If Riya changes her reaction, nothing more is paid. The full list is under **How posts pay** on the cover.

## Reactions, replies and mentions

- **React:** tap **Pick a reaction** under a post and choose Like, Celebrate, Support, Love, Insightful or Funny. Tapping the same one again takes it back.
- **Reply:** tap the speech bubble with the reply count. A sheet opens. Type in **Write a reply** and tap **Send**. Tap **Reply** under someone''s reply to answer them. Answers nest up to four levels deep.
- **Mention:** type @ and a page name, like @arjun_cuts. That person is told. A post can tell up to five people.
- **Follow:** open a post''s **More** menu and pick **Follow the post** to hear about new replies. The writer and everyone who replies follow it on their own.

Use **Show**, **Sort** and **Topic** above the feed to filter. Show is Everyone, Following or Mine. Sort is Recent, Popular or Most replies.

## Anonymous posts

Riya wants advice on a client who will not pay. She does not want her name on it.

1. She taps **Write a post** and picks **Support**.
2. She ticks **Post anonymously**.
3. She taps **Create post**.

Readers see a made-up name, the same all through that one thread and different in the next. They never see her name, page, followers or rank. An anonymous post never shows under Following, on a public page, or in a search by name. Staff keep the link to her account, so a report can be acted on. She can still delete her own post, because the app knows it is hers.

## Channels

Channels are chat rooms, one topic each, beside the feed.

1. Riya taps **Channels**. A dot shows rooms with lines she has not read.
2. She opens a room and taps **Join** with the room''s name.
3. She writes in the box at the bottom. She can tick **Post anonymously** here too.
4. Under any line, **Reply** answers it. **More** gives **Report**, **Block** and, for a guide, **Mute**.

**Leave** takes her out, and she can join again later.

Only a guide opens a room. A guide taps **New channel**, fills in **Name** and **What it is for (optional)**, and taps **Make the channel**. Inside a room, a guide has **Edit** and **Close channel**. A closed room can still be read, and **Open again** brings it back. Guides can tick **Show closed channels** in the list.

## Guides and ranks

A guide is anyone who holds the **Moderation** permission. A creator can hold it, so Riya can become one.

There are four ranks: member, guide, staff and admin. A guide can mute or hide only someone of a lower rank. Undoing it needs the same rank or a higher one. So a guide acts on members, staff act on guides and members, and an admin acts on everyone except another admin.

## What a guide does

Once Riya is a guide, she sees more on the Feed.

- **For guides:** a switch, **Hold a new member''s first post until a guide says okay**. It is off by default. While it is on, a new member''s first post is seen only by them until a guide taps **Say okay**. Only then does it pay, and its mentions are told.
- **Pin:** in a post''s **More** menu. Up to three posts are pinned at the top of each category.
- **Hide:** in **More**, asked first in a sheet. The post leaves the feed for everyone, and **Unhide** brings it back.
- **Mute:** in **More**, asked first. The person can read but cannot post or reply. **Muted members** lists them, and **Lift** ends a mute.
- **Take down:** in a channel, under a line. A takedown by someone else is audited and wipes the words.

A report on a channel line becomes a help ticket with the line''s words. Every post, reply and line shows web addresses as plain text, never as links.

> **Pro tip:** before making someone a guide, read a few of their posts. A good guide is calm, replies often, and never argues in public.

> **Result:** Riya posts, reacts and earns small amounts each day, asks a hard question without her name, and, once trusted, helps keep Community kind.

## What the admin checks

1. **Make a guide:** open **Admin**, then **People**. Search for Riya and tap her row. Under **Permissions**, tap **Moderation**. Taking a permission away asks first in a sheet.
2. **The queue:** a staff member with Moderation sees a **Moderation** tile on **Manage** that opens Community. A creator guide does all of it from Community itself.
3. **Switches:** in **Admin**, **Flags**, the Community row sets **On**, **Minimum role**, **Rollout %** and **Overrides**. In **People**, **What does this person see?** sets it for one person.
4. **Audit:** every role and permission change is in **Audit**.

## The bottom line

Community works when people feel safe to speak. Teach members to post, react and reply, and to use anonymous posts for the hard things. As an admin, choose guides with care, give them Moderation, and let the rank rules keep every guide in their lane.', 13, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l13', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Bond: friends, codes, chats and blocks', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya met an editor called Arjun at a meetup. They want to swap tips, maybe share a client. Bond lets them become friends, talk, and stay safe while they do. Nothing moves in Bond until both people say yes.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How usernames and friend codes work.
- How Riya adds a friend and answers a request.
- How anonymous chats and private channels differ.
- How introductions after an event work.
- How blocks work, and what an admin can check.

## Switch it on

Bond is off on a new account. Riya opens **Apps**, taps the **Connection** group and turns on the switch beside **Bond**.

## Usernames and friend codes

Every account has two things that find it.

- **Username:** her page address, like @riya_edits. A new account gets one that starts with user_ and a few letters, so it is never blank. She changes it in the **Public page** app, in the **Username** field. It takes three to twenty letters, numbers or underscores, and nobody else can have it.
- **Friend code:** eight letters and numbers, shown on Bond. It is only for adding friends.

## Step by step: adding Arjun

1. Riya opens **Bond** and scrolls to **Friends**.
2. Under **Your friend code** she taps **Copy** and sends the code to Arjun.
3. Arjun opens Bond, types it in **Add a friend by code** and taps **Send request**.
4. On Riya''s Bond, a card says "Arjun would like to be friends". She taps **Yes**.
5. Arjun now shows in her list as **Friends**, with a **Chat** button.

There is a second way. Riya opens someone''s public page and taps **Add friend**. If they asked her first, the button reads **Accept request**. A request she sent shows as "Request sent, waiting" with **Cancel**. **Remove** ends a friendship. Both ask first in a sheet.

## Chats under aliases

A chat in Bond always runs under two made-up names. Nobody''s real name moves through it, even between friends.

1. Riya taps **Chat** beside Arjun.
2. The chat opens under her alias and his.
3. She writes and sends. The chat shows under **Chats** at the top of Bond.

A chat can also start from someone at her circle table, or from a person''s page with **Chat anonymously**. There is no reveal button. That is the rule.

## Channels for friends

A channel is different: a small private room where names show.

1. Under **Channels**, Riya taps **New channel**.
2. She names it "Pune editors" and taps **Make the channel**.
3. Inside it she taps **Ask friends** and taps **Ask** beside Arjun and two others.
4. Each of them sees "You were asked into this channel" with **Say yes** or **No thanks**.

Only accepted friends can be asked. A room holds up to twelve people, counting the ones asked, and Riya can own up to five rooms. As the owner she can take someone out with the small cross by their name, take down any line, and **Close channel**. Anyone else can **Leave**. A person not in the room cannot open it at all. Nobody is anonymous in a Bond channel.

## Introductions after an event

Bond also helps people meet the ones they crossed paths with.

1. When Riya signs up for a Party event, she ticks **Introduce me to other guests**.
2. On Bond, under **Introductions**, she turns on **Open to introductions**.
3. People from her events who said yes show under **People you met**.
4. She taps **Introduce** beside one, writes **Why you would like to meet**, and taps **Send the request**.
5. They see her name and her note, and tap **Yes, introduce us** or **No thanks**.

Her details only move after the other person says yes.

## Blocks

Riya can block anyone, any time. She finds **Block** in many places: a post''s **More** menu in Community, a channel line''s **More**, a reply, or an introduction card.

A block works both ways:

- **Friendship:** it ends.
- **Posts and replies:** neither sees the other''s.
- **Event and channel lines:** hidden both ways.
- **The leaderboard:** neither shows on the other''s board.
- **Asks:** neither can send the other a friend request or an introduction.

The blocked person is not told. Blocking from an introduction offers a **Reason, for the team only** box. Riya can lift a block any time under **People you blocked** with **Unblock**, which asks first.

## Her page''s switches

On the **Public page** app, under **What is public**, two switches shape Bond:

- **Let people add me as a friend:** off hides the Add friend button.
- **Let friends start a chat:** off means nobody can open a chat with her.

The server enforces both, not only the screen.

> **Pro tip:** tell new members to share their friend code, not their phone number. A code adds a friend and gives nothing else away.

> **Result:** Riya has Arjun as a friend, a private room with three editors, and a chat that never shows her name. She knows she can block anyone in one tap.

## What the admin checks

- **Anonymity:** chats and circle members are aliases only. Member ids never reach the browser, and there is no reveal, for you either.
- **Deleted accounts:** a deleted member never shows in friends or requests.
- **Switches:** in **Admin**, **Flags**, the Bond row sets **On**, **Minimum role**, **Rollout %** and **Overrides**. In **People**, tap a row, then **What does this person see?** to set it for one person.
- **Reports:** a reported channel line becomes a help ticket with the line''s words, in the support queue.
- **Public page:** the friend and chat switches are the member''s own choice. Do not ask them to turn them on.

If someone says they cannot add a friend, check three things: Bond is on for both, the other person allows friend requests, and neither has blocked the other. A block reads the same as an unknown person, on purpose.

## The bottom line

Bond is built on consent. Friends need a yes, introductions need a yes, and channels need a yes. Teach members to use friend codes and to block without guilt. As an admin, protect the aliases and leave the privacy switches to each person.', 14, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l14', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Party, Run and Circles: events, codes and meeting people', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya spends her days at a screen. She wants to meet creators in Pune, and maybe run again. Party, Kreators Run and Circles get people out of the house, and one rule ties them together: the attendee code.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya hosts a meetup and checks guests in.
- How attendee codes pay xp, and who can see them.
- How a run club works, from the start code to Discussions.
- How Circles seat people under aliases.
- What the team runs, and what an admin checks.

## Switch them on

All three are off on a new account. Riya opens **Apps**, taps the **Connection** group and turns on **Party**, **Kreators Run** and **Circles**.

## Step by step: Riya hosts a meetup

1. She opens **Party** and taps **Host a meetup**.
2. Under **What kind** she picks **Meetup**. Party, Workshop, Camp and Kreators Day are run by the team.
3. She fills in **Title** ("Editors'' chai, Koregaon Park"), **What to expect**, **Starts** and **Minutes**.
4. She types a **Venue**. Left empty, it is an online event with an **Online link**.
5. She sets **Seats** to 12. Blank means no limit.
6. She leaves **Price in points** at 0.
7. Under **Who can see it** she picks **Everyone**. The other choices are **Club members** and **People with the link**.
8. Under **Xp for each guest, optional**, she types 20 in **Xp** and leaves **Your own code** empty for a random one.
9. She taps **Publish event**.

Guests get a reminder a day and an hour before.

## Guests sign up

Arjun opens the event. He ticks **Introduce me to other guests** and taps **RSVP**. A priced event reads **RSVP for** and the points. A full room reads **Join the waitlist**. If a seat opens, the first person waiting gets it and a note. **Cancel my RSVP** gives back any points paid.

## The attendee code

Only the host, events staff and admins ever see the code.

- **When it works:** from thirty minutes before the start to three hours after the end.
- **Who can use it:** only the event''s own guests, once each.
- **How often:** up to three events in any 24 hours per person.
- **The xp:** up to 50. It is fixed once the code opens. The code itself can change any time.

On the event page, Riya''s card shows the code, the xp and "0 of 12 guests claimed it". **Set xp**, **Use it** (her own word, four to twelve letters or digits) and **New random code** change it. At the door she shows the code. Arjun types it in **The host''s code** and taps **Get 20 xp**. A Party claim also checks him in. Riya can also tap **Check in** beside a guest''s name.

## After the event

- **Mark done** closes it. **Cancel event** asks first, then tells everyone.
- **Run it again** opens a date field and **Make the new event**. The xp carries over with a fresh code. Guests do not.
- Guests rate it under **How was it?** and tap **Send feedback**.
- Under **Photos**, the host shares one folder link with **Share the folder**. It must be Google Drive or Photos, Dropbox, iCloud, OneDrive or WeTransfer.
- **Meet other guests** lists those who ticked **List me so other guests can connect with me**. **Connect** sends a friend request.

## Kreators Run

Riya starts a club.

1. She opens **Kreators Run** and taps **Start a club**.
2. She fills in **Name**, **City**, **Pace**, **When and where you usually meet** and **About the club**.
3. Under **Who can find it** she picks **Everyone** or **People with the link**.
4. She taps **Start the club**. She is its host and first member.
5. On the club page she taps **Add a run**, sets **Title**, **Starts**, **Minutes**, **Kilometres** and **Meeting point**, and taps **Add the run**. **Xp for each runner** is 20 unless she sets it, up to 50.

Each run gets a start code only the host sees: six letters and numbers, or a word she picks. A member taps **Enter the start code**, types it in **Log this run**, and adds kilometres and minutes. A logged run pays its xp and 10 points, up to two paid runs a day. Its minutes count as movement in Life. Riya never logs her own run.

The app has four sections: **Clubs**, **My runs**, **Brand collabs** and **Discussions**.

- **My runs:** kilometres over thirty days, a weekly streak, and badges.
- **Brand collabs:** posted by events staff or a brand''s own team. Only a host applies, with **Apply with a club**.
- **Discussions:** one per run, for its host, club members and runners who logged it. Open for thirty days after the run. A line can be anonymous.

## Circles

Circles are small weekly tables, run by the team.

1. Riya opens **Circles**, finds this week''s in Pune and taps **Join**.
2. A sheet asks **How will you show up?**: Single (wear something red), Committed (something white) or Rather not say.
3. She taps **Join this week''s circle**. Signups close thirty-six hours before.

Two days before, tables are set from interests that fit. Everyone has an alias and keeps it. The table chat opens a day before, and they agree on the place. At the meetup she types the code on her table page, from thirty minutes before to five hours after the start.

Staff with the Events permission see **Circles you run**. They tap **Open a circle**, fill in **Title**, **City**, **Area (optional)**, **Starts**, **People per table** and **Venue hint**, and can turn on the weekly switch. **Open it** saves it. **Set the tables now** works once three people sign up. A circle''s host sees counts, never names.

> **Pro tip:** set the xp when you make the event. Once the code opens, thirty minutes before the start, the xp is fixed.

> **Result:** Riya hosts a chai meetup with a code at the door, runs on Sundays with a club she started, and sits at a weekly table under an alias.

## What the admin checks

1. **Event hosts:** give **Events** in **Admin**, **People**, under **Permissions**. Events needs the Staff role.
2. **Codes:** **Admin**, **Attendee codes** lists every Party, Run and Circles event with guests, from a week ago on. You can set any xp or code by the same rules. The host is told, and it is audited.
3. **Switches:** in **Flags**, each app''s row sets **On**, **Minimum role**, **Rollout %** and **Overrides**.

If a code fails, check the time window and the guest list.

## The bottom line

Events pay only when people show up. Teach hosts to set xp early and show the code at the door. As an admin, give Events to the people who run the big days, and use Attendee codes when a host needs help.', 15, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l15', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Games, Rewards, badges and the Leaderboard', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya has been checking in for three weeks. Her points are piling up. What are they for? This lesson covers the four places that turn showing up into something real: Rewards, the Games app, badges and the Leaderboard.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya earns points and spends them in Rewards.
- How a card game is unlocked and played.
- How badges are earned and which ones stay private.
- How the Leaderboard works and how to opt in.
- What an admin can give by hand, and how to handle a reservation.

## Points and xp

These are two different things.

- **Xp:** shows effort. Ticks, posts and events pay it. It moves your level and the Leaderboard.
- **Points:** can be spent. A check-in pays 10 points, and every seventh day in a row pays 50 extra. Likes on your posts and new followers pay a few more.

Riya sees both on the **Profile** tab, under **Points**, with two buttons: **Rewards** and **Rank**.

## Step by step: Riya''s first reward

1. On the **Profile** tab, under **Points**, she taps **Rewards**.
2. **Your balance** shows her points at the top.
3. She scrolls the **Catalog**. Its shelves are Books, Games, Apparel and wearables, Tech accessories, Drinkware, Office and stationery, Lifestyle and wellness, Novelty and fun, and Experiences.
4. Under **Books** she finds The Relationship Method, 2,000 points. She taps **Reserve 2,000 pts**.
5. A sheet asks again before any points move. She says yes.
6. The book now shows under **Your reservations**, above the catalog.

Gear is a reservation until a drop ships. **Cancel** on a reservation gives the points back, and asks first.

## Card games

The **Games** shelf holds nine card games, such as FunFlow, HealFlow and the 7RP Challenge. A game is different from gear.

- **It opens at once:** the game is hers for good. Nothing is posted.
- **It cannot be cancelled:** the points are spent.
- **It may have a lock:** some games need some xp first. A locked one shows a padlock and says what is missing, like "Unlocks at 500 xp".

Riya unlocks the 7RP Challenge for 1,000 points.

1. She taps **Unlock 1,000 pts** and says yes in the sheet.
2. The **Games** app switches itself on.
3. She opens **Games**. Her game wears **Yours**, and **Play** opens it.

## Step by step: playing a game

1. At the card table, she picks a deck from the row of decks.
2. She taps **Draw a card**. Each tap on the pile deals another.
3. She taps a card to flip it. Some cards have two sides, like Truth and Dare.
4. She drags a card to move it. The card she touches comes to the front.
5. **Shuffle**, **Flip all** and **Clear table** sit beside Draw a card.
6. **Focus mode**, the button with four corners, hides the menu and fills the screen. Escape leaves it.

## Badges

Badges are medals the app gives by its own count. Riya finds them on the **Profile** tab, under **Badges**.

- **Ten families:** Daily habits, Pillars, Focus, Reflection, Community, Creator, Money, Fitness, Learning and Milestones.
- **Four tiers:** bronze, silver, gold and platinum.
- **One rule each:** a single count reaching a number, like ten projects cleared.
- **Kept for good:** once earned, a badge is never taken back.
- **Private families:** Reflection, Focus and Money show only to her. They never appear on her page, the Leaderboard count or an unlock.

Tapping a badge opens a sheet with its next step and a bar. A badge pays nothing. Some products, store items, tools and games ask for a badge before they open.

Honors are different. The team gives them, or time in the app unlocks them. They show under **Awarded**.

## The Leaderboard

The Leaderboard is on for everyone, but nobody shows on it until they say so.

1. On the **Profile** tab, under **Profile**, Riya ticks **Show me on the leaderboard**.
2. She taps **Save profile**.
3. She opens **Leaderboard** (or **Rank** under Points).
4. Under **Board** she picks Everyone, By goal, By pillar, By city, By who they are or By experience.
5. Under **Rank by** she picks All time, This month, Streak or Badges.

Her spot and her xp sit at the top. Below are **Boards by goal** and **Boards by pillar**, each with its top three. A pillar board ranks only the xp earned in that pillar.

The rules:

- **Opt in and setup:** she shows only after she opts in and finishes setup.
- **Blocks:** nobody she blocked, and nobody who blocked her, is listed.
- **Never counted:** journal and wellbeing never count, and her date of birth is never used.
- **Seasons:** each month is a season. Its top three get the **Season top three** honor.

> **Pro tip:** tell new members to check in daily for a week before they spend. The seventh day pays the bonus, and a card game is often their best first reward.

> **Result:** Riya reserved a book, plays a card game with friends, has her first badges, and sees where she stands among editors in Pune.

## What the admin checks

1. **Reservations:** **Admin**, **Redemptions** lists what people reserved. Points are held until you tick rows and pick **Mark fulfilled**, or **Cancel and refund**, which asks first.
2. **Give by hand:** in **People**, tap a row. Under **Points**, **XP** and **Badges given by hand**, type an amount or pick a badge, then **Add** or **Give**. A minus in XP takes some away, down to zero. Under **Honors**, pick one and **Grant**. Each keeps a note in the audit log.
3. **Taking back:** a badge you gave can be removed, which asks first. One the person earned is never taken away.
4. **Super admins:** an admin is never locked out of a game, product, item or tool.
5. **Switches:** in **Flags**, the Games row sets **On**, **Minimum role**, **Rollout %** and **Overrides**.

## The bottom line

Points and badges reward showing up, not gaming the system. Teach members to opt in to the Leaderboard if they want, and to spend points on things they will use. As an admin, fulfil reservations on time and give by hand only with a note.', 16, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l16', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Shop, Store, Tools and Content: selling and making', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya cuts reels that keep people watching, and other creators keep asking how. Here she turns that skill into a course, sells sessions, lists her old camera and plans content for a brand.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya makes a product and a course in the Shop.
- How a course moves through Draft, Private, Public and Archived, with invites.
- How coupons and bookings work.
- How the Store, Tools and Content apps help her.
- What an admin can see and change.

## The Shop

The Shop is on for a new account. Its sections are **Overview**, **My page**, **Customers**, **Bookings**, **Content**, **Priority DMs**, **Reviews**, **Audience** and **Auto-DM**. Points work today. Money checkout is in test mode until a payment provider is connected, and a money order is paid only once the provider confirms it.

First, Riya picks her page name under **My page**, so people can find her products.

## Step by step: Riya''s course

1. On **Overview**, she taps **New product**.
2. Under **What are you selling?** she picks **Course**. Other kinds are Download, Membership, Session, Event, Link, Payment link, Bundle, Locked post and Priority DM.
3. She types the **Title**, "Reels that hold", and a **Price in points**. Zero makes it free.
4. She taps **Create draft**. The product page opens.
5. Under **Lessons** she taps **Add lesson**, or **Draft from notes** to turn an outline into draft lessons. Each lesson has text, an optional video link, a drip day and an optional quiz.
6. In the form she can add a money price and set **Who can buy**: **Xp of at least**, **Points of at least** and **Holds the badge**. Empty means anyone.

## The four states of a course

At the top of the product page, **Who can see it** shows the state. A course has four:

- **Draft:** only Riya can see it. Nobody can buy it yet.
- **Private:** only the people she invites can see it and enrol. It is in no list, search or public page, and never in a bundle.
- **Public:** anyone can find it and enrol.
- **Archived:** hidden from everyone, and nobody new can buy it. Buyers keep what they have.

Every other kind has three: Draft, Live and Archived.

Riya wants three students to test her course first.

1. She taps **Private**.
2. **Invite people** opens. She types an email, @username or friend code and taps **Invite**. Each person gets an inbox note.
3. Later she taps **Public**, or **Publish** while it is a draft.
4. **Remove** takes an invite back, after a sheet asks. Anyone who already enrolled keeps what they have.

When she makes a new version, she taps **Archived** on the old one. **Delete for good** appears in the **More** menu only once it is archived, and asks first. A product someone has bought is never deleted; it stays archived so their order and access stay.

## Coupons and bookings

- **Coupons:** on the product page, type a **Code** like LAUNCH20 and a **% off**, then **Add**. **Remove** asks first. A buyer can also type a team discount code; Riya is still paid the full price.
- **Sessions:** a Session product sets its length, buffer, notice and how far ahead people can book. Under **Bookings**, **Availability** holds her weekly windows: a day, **From** and **To**, then **Add**. She can block a date or give it its own hours. A request shows with **Confirm**, which takes an optional meeting link, and **Decline**, which asks first.

## The Store

The Store is off on a new account. It has three shelves: new gear picked by the team, pre-owned gear from creators, and kits by budget.

1. Riya taps **Sell something** to list her old camera.
2. She fills in **What is it**, **Category**, **Condition**, **Asking price**, and optional extras like **What comes with it** and picture links.
3. She taps **List it**.
4. A buyer taps her listing, types an amount and taps **Make the offer**.
5. Riya taps **Accept** or **Decline**. A chat opens for the handover.
6. When it is done she taps **Mark as sold**.

The two of them settle the money and the handover; kreators.app takes nothing. **Feature for 7 days · 200 points** puts her listing at the top for a week. She can have up to twenty open listings.

New gear goes out through a partner link, and the card says "Partner link. Kreators may earn a share."

## Tools

Tools is a directory checked by the team. **Get it** goes through a partner link, **Open** goes straight to the tool. Riya taps **Suggest a tool**, fills in **Name**, **Link**, **Category** and **What it is good for**, and sends it. She sees it at once; everyone else sees it after a reviewer checks it.

## Content: the strategist

Riya edits for a chai brand. Content gives her a folder for it.

1. She opens **Content** and taps **New brand**.
2. She fills in **Brand**, **Niche**, **What it is** and **How it sounds**, and taps **Make the folder**.
3. Under **Links** she adds the brand''s site and channels, and ticks **This is a competitor** for rivals.
4. Under **Research** she picks a kind and taps **Run it**.
5. Under **Create** she picks a piece, like Hooks or Four-week calendar, and writes the brief. **Research first** reads unread links before writing.

Eight strategy steps guide the order, from the brand''s voice to a month of posts. Only that folder goes to the model, never her journal or customers.

> **Pro tip:** keep a new course Private for a week with a few invited testers. Fix what confuses them, then make it Public.

> **Result:** Riya has a course her testers finished, sessions people book from her hours, a camera sold to another editor, and a month of posts planned for her client.

## What the admin checks

1. **Every course:** an admin sees and changes every product of every creator, in every state. A change to someone else''s product goes to the audit log.
2. **Store shelves:** staff see **Team actions** on the Store, with **Add a product** and **Make a kit**.
3. **Tools:** a reviewer''s **More** menu on a tool has **Verify**, **Mark as partner link**, **Feature for a week** and **Remove**.
4. **Discount codes:** made under **Admin**, **Discount codes**, which needs the Finance permission.
5. **Orders:** **Admin**, **Orders** shows each order with a timeline and a refund.
6. **Switches:** in **Flags**, each app''s row sets **On**, **Minimum role**, **Rollout %** and **Overrides**.

## The bottom line

The Shop lets a creator start small and grow: draft, test with invites, open to all, archive when done. Teach creators to use Private before Public. As an admin, you can open any course in any state, so use that power to help, and let the audit log show it.', 17, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l17', 'prog-admin-guide', 'School, Work, Army and Win: learning and earning', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya is good, but she wants to be better and paid more. Four apps in the Contribution group help: School to learn, Work to find gigs, Army for brand campaigns and Win for content rewards. This lesson follows her through each, then shows what the team runs behind them.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya enrols in a cohort and hands in work.
- How the team makes a program and sets its state.
- How Work, Army and Win move from brief to payment.
- Which permission runs each step, and what an admin checks.

## School: Riya the learner

School, Work and Army are on for a new account. Win is off until switched on.

1. Riya opens **School**. Under **Programs** she finds Editing fundamentals.
2. Beside a cohort date she taps **Enrol**. A program priced in points asks again in a sheet before spending.
3. Under **Your cohorts** she taps **Open**.
4. Under **Lessons** she opens each one in order. A lesson shows its words, then **Watch video**, **Open resource** or **Download resource** at its foot.
5. Under **Assignments** she reads the brief, adds **Link to your work** or writes in **Or write it here**, and taps **Hand in**.
6. Under **Live sessions**, **I am here** checks her in while a session is live. Check-in opens fifteen minutes before.
7. In **Discussion** she starts a thread with **Start a thread** and **Post**. She picks Private, so her handle stays hidden, or Public, so others can open her page.

A mentor leaves feedback and taps **Approve** or **Ask for changes**. Completion is the cohort manager''s decision against the criteria, never a timer. When the manager marks her complete, the cohort page says Completed. Well done.

## School: the team''s side

Making programs needs the **Cohorts** permission (cohort manager). Giving feedback needs **Mentor**, which a creator like Riya can hold.

1. A cohort manager taps **New program** on School.
2. They fill in **Name**, **Description**, **Price in points**, **How it runs**, **What people leave with** and **What they need before starting**.
3. Under **Who can see it** they pick Draft, Private or Public, then tap **Create program**.
4. In the program''s menu: **Add lesson**, **Add assignment** and **Open a cohort**. Whoever opens a cohort becomes its manager, scheduling sessions, giving feedback and marking completion.

A program has four states:

- **Draft:** only its maker, its cohort managers and admins see it.
- **Private:** only invited people see it and enrol. Its maker picks **Invite people** and adds an email, @username or friend code.
- **Public:** listed on School and Explore.
- **Archived:** hidden; no one new enrols, and people already in keep their cohort.

The menu moves it with **Make it public**, **Make it private (invite only)**, **Move it back to draft** and **Archive**. Taking it off the public list or archiving asks first. Only an archived program shows **Delete for good**, which asks once more and takes its cohorts, lessons, hand-ins and discussions with it.

## Work

1. Riya opens **Work** and finds a gig from a brand.
2. She taps **Apply**, writes **A short note** and taps **Send application**. Her public portfolio goes with it, one application per person.
3. Each change, from submitted to shortlisted, selected or declined, lands in her inbox. **Withdraw** takes it back, after a sheet asks.

Brands and staff with **Talent** tap **Post one**. The form ends in **Save as draft**. **Publish** from **Your listings** puts it live, **Close** ends it, and **Back to draft** pulls it back. **Club only** shows it to Club members first. On each applicant, the poster picks reviewing, shortlisted, selected or rejected, with an optional **Note to send with a decision**.

## Army

Army is brand campaigns run properly.

1. A brand taps **New brand**. Its owner can add teammates by email.
2. They tap **New campaign** and fill in the brief, budget, dates, **Usage rights**, **Exclusivity** and **Restrictions**, then **Send to the team**.
3. The team moves it under **Move it along**: New lead, Brief written, Scope and budget, Creators shortlisted, Approved by brand, In production, In review, Delivered, then Reported and invoiced. The brand can approve the shortlist or cancel.
4. Staff tap **Assign** to offer Riya a role and a fee.
5. Riya taps **Upload a version** with a link. The brand taps **Approve** or **Request changes** with a comment.

**Show on the public site** lists a campaign under Campaigns. The brief itself stays private.

## Win

Win is content rewards: a brand funds a pool of points and pays per thousand verified views.

1. Riya switches **Win** on, opens a reward and reads the brief.
2. **What could I earn?** shows the payout for any view count.
3. She posts her video, taps **Enter**, pastes the link and taps **Submit my video**.
4. YouTube views are read by the app. Other platforms are typed in and confirmed by a reviewer, one report every six hours.

A reviewer types **Confirmed views** and taps **Verify** or **Reject**. Finance then taps **Pay** with the amount, which asks first. Nothing pays on a claim. A pool that runs dry pays nobody more. If a reward changes after launch, its version goes up and everyone who entered is told.

> **Pro tip:** before giving someone Mentor, enrol them in a cohort yourself and read their feedback on one hand-in.

> **Result:** Riya finished a cohort, got shortlisted for a gig, delivered an Army campaign and earned points from a Win reward, each step with someone checking it.

## What the admin checks

1. **Permissions:** in **Admin**, **People**, tap a row. **Cohorts**, **Talent**, **Review** and **Finance** need the Staff role. **Mentor** can go to a creator.
2. **Every program:** on School, an admin sees **Open to me** and **Every program**. Every program shows each program from anyone, in every state, with who made it, and you can change any of them.
3. **Queues:** **Manage** shows tiles for Talent, Cohorts, Mentor, Review and Finance, with what is waiting.
4. **Listings:** **Admin**, **Listings** sets what Explore shows.

## The bottom line

Every path here has a person checking it: a mentor, a reviewer, finance. Teach members to enrol, apply and enter. As an admin, give each permission to someone who will use it well, and use Every program to see the whole of School.', 18, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l18', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Agency: clients, pipeline and paperwork', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya edits videos in Pune. She makes her own content, and brands pay her to make videos too. Her clients lived in chats and a folder of invoices. Agency puts them in one place.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya sets up her business details once.
- How she adds a client, moves them through the stages and sends paperwork.
- How the client link, the team link and the team work.
- What you, as an admin, can check and change.

## Switch Agency on

Agency is off on a new account. It sits in the Contribution group.

1. Riya opens the Apps tab and opens the Contribution group.
2. She turns on the switch beside Agency.
3. She taps Agency to open it.

The screen has five tabs: **Overview, Pipeline, Clients, Paperwork and Team**. A round gear at the top opens **Agency settings**.

## Set up the business once

Riya types her business only once. Every invoice, agreement and note is filled from it.

1. She taps the gear to open Agency settings.
2. She types her details right on the sample invoice and agreement paper: her name, business name, address, emails, phones and website.
3. Under **Packages and pricing** she types what a package counts, like Videos, and a row for each package with its count and fee.
4. Under **Signatures and courts** she adds her designation and the city for courts.
5. She opens **Currency and hours** and sets her currency and the hours she works in a week.

It saves as she types. A quiet line says Saved, with Undo.

## Add a client

1. On Overview or Clients, Riya taps **Add client**.
2. In **Add a client** she types the brand name and the contact''s email.
3. She sets a **Password for their page**, or taps **Generate one**.
4. She opens **More details** for the retainer a month, deliverables a month, hours and billing.
5. She saves. The password shows once, so she copies it now.

The app never sends a password. Riya shares it with the brand another way, like a call or a message.

## Move a client through the stages

A client goes through nine stages: **Enquiry, Agreement sent, Agreement signed, Strategy session, Trial video, Pilot month, Retainer proposed, Retainer active and Monthly delivery.**

1. Riya opens the client. The stage card sits at the top.
2. It says what the client does now and what she does now.
3. When a step is done, she taps **Move to** and the next stage''s name.
4. **Stage details** folds open the checklist.

Sending or signing an agreement moves the stage forward by itself. The app never moves a client back on its own.

The client page has four parts: **Work, Money, Paper and Link**.

## Plan the work in the pipeline

1. On the Pipeline tab, Riya taps **Add deliverable**.
2. Each piece goes through **Idea, Script, Making, In review, Approved and Published**.
3. A switch picks **List** or **Board**. On the board she drags a card, or taps its Move button and picks a column.

## Send paperwork

Paperwork has three parts: **Invoices, Agreements and Documents**.

1. Riya taps **New agreement**, picks the client and a template: built in, her own or a house template.
2. The paper opens. She edits it right on the page, and it saves as she types.
3. In the More menu she taps **Fill in the details** to fill every blank in one list.
4. She taps **Send to client**. The brand reads it at their link and accepts by typing their name.

The screen says plainly that a typed name is not a certified e-signature.

For money, she taps **New invoice**, fills the lines, then **Send to client**. When the brand pays, she taps **Mark paid**. A sent invoice can be voided from More, and **Restore** brings it back with the same number.

## The client link and the team link

The **Link** part holds the client link.

- **Copy link** copies it. **Switch it on** opens it again when it is off.
- **What your client sees** sets which parts show and what they can do.
- More has **Make a fresh link** and **Switch the link off**.

A new client first sees a short form: legal name, brands, address, PIN code, state, tax numbers and contact. When they send it, their details land on the record and the trial agreement is made.

The brand''s junior staff get the **team link**. Riya taps **Switch the team link on** and must set a password first. It never shows invoices, agreements, money or private notes.

If a brand loses its password, it can ask for a new one. Riya then sees **Set a new password** as the page''s main button.

## Offer a retainer

1. After the pilot, on the client''s **Money** part, Riya taps **Offer a retainer**.
2. She offers up to four lengths, each from 1 to 24 months, with an optional discount.
3. The brand picks one at their link.
4. Riya taps **Confirm and start**.
5. **Send each month''s invoice by itself** decides whether each month''s invoice goes out or waits as a draft.

## Bring in a team

1. On the Team tab, Riya taps **Invite member**.
2. She types an email, @username or friend code.
3. She picks a role: **Content creator, Videographer, Video editor or Client**.
4. Under **What they can open**, she sets each tab to Off, View or Edit.
5. She taps **Send invite**.

A team can hold up to 25 people. A member never sees her bank, fees, private notes or the client link.

## What an admin checks

- **Allowing or blocking it:** in Admin, People, tap a row and use **What does this person see?** to allow or block Agency for that person. For a whole brand, add an override on Agency under Flags, for a brand. An override never switches it on: the person then turns it on from **Apps** (Contribution), as under Switch Agency on.
- **House templates:** only a super admin saves one, from their own paper, with **Save as house template** in More. They are listed under the **Agency templates** tab.
- **Counts only:** Observability shows how much Agency is used. It never shows client names, amounts or notes.
- **Owner only:** no staff member or admin can open Riya''s clients, fees or notes.

> **Pro tip:** ask a new Agency user to fill Agency settings first. Every paper after that fills itself, and the "Not filled yet" marks go away.

> **Result:** Riya runs each brand from first enquiry to monthly delivery, with paperwork, passwords and a team in one place.

## The bottom line

Agency is Riya''s business desk. She types her business once, adds a client with a password, moves them stage by stage and sends paper that fills itself. You keep it switched on for the right people and never need to see what is inside.', 19, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l19', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Finance: money in, money out and what is owed', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya gets paid by brands, sells a small course and pays an editor friend to help on busy weeks. At the end of each month she could never say what she kept. Finance answers that in one screen, and only she can see it.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How Riya picks her currency and logs money in and out.
- How she tracks what is owed, what repeats, her budgets and her goals.
- How Agency invoices show up in Finance.
- What an admin can and cannot see here.

## Switch Finance on

Finance is off on a new account. It sits in the Creation group.

1. Riya opens the Apps tab and opens the Creation group.
2. She turns on the switch beside Finance.
3. She taps Finance to open it.

The cover shows four numbers for all time: **earned, spent, kept and platform points**. Under them are two solid buttons, **Money in** and **Money out**. Under those sit four quiet ones: **Owed, Monthly, Goal and Budgets**.

## Pick a currency first

Money is kept in one currency for each person. It is rupees unless the person changes it.

1. Riya opens the Public page app. Its screen is called Profile.
2. She finds **Currency** and picks one from the list.
3. She taps **Save profile**.

Finance adds up only that currency. An entry in another currency is listed, but never added in and never converted. The screen says so under the entries.

## Log money in

1. Riya taps **Money in**.
2. She types the **Amount** and **From whom**, like the brand''s name.
3. She picks a **Category**: Brand deal, Content reward, Courses, Products, Sessions, Affiliate, Ad revenue or Other income.
4. She checks the **Date** and adds a **Note** if she likes.
5. Under **Splits (optional)** she adds her editor''s share. A split stays owed until she marks it paid.
6. She taps **Save**.

## Log money out

1. Riya taps **Money out**.
2. She types the amount and **To whom**.
3. She picks a category: Equipment, Software, Travel, Team, Marketing, Fees, Taxes or Other expense.
4. She taps **Save**.

## Track what is owed

1. Riya taps **Owed**.
2. She picks **Someone owes me** or **I owe someone**.
3. She types **Who**, the **Amount**, **Due by** and a **Note**.
4. She saves it.

It now shows under **Owed**. When the money moves, she taps **Received** or **Paid** on that row. Settled ones fold away under **Settled**. **Remove** asks first.

If Agency is on, her sent invoices show here too, as **Owed to you, invoices**. When she marks an invoice paid in Agency, it counts as income.

## Set what repeats each month

1. Riya taps **Monthly**. The sheet is called **Repeats monthly**.
2. She adds her software plan: the amount, the **Day of the month** and a note.
3. It is posted on that day, every month, under **Repeats monthly**.

## Set budgets

1. Riya taps **Budgets**. The sheet is called **Monthly budgets**.
2. She types a number beside each kind of spending she wants to watch, like Software or Travel.
3. She taps **Save budgets**.

The **Budgets** part shows how each one is going this month.

## Save for a goal

1. Riya taps **Goal**. The sheet is called **New goal**.
2. Under **What for** she types "A new camera".
3. She sets a **Target** and, if she likes, a day under **By (optional)**.
4. Later, on the goal, she types an amount to put aside and taps **Add**.

## Read the month

The month view has arrows to go back and forward. It shows **in, out, net and platform points**, bars over recent months and a **By category** chart. **Entries** lists every line for the month. The **By period** block shows the same numbers for a week, a month, a quarter or a half year.

Riya can also use the assistant. Its free flows include **Add money in or out**, **Note money owed**, **Settle something owed**, **Set a money goal** and **Add to a money goal**.

## What an admin checks

- **It is private:** Finance is the person''s own. Staff and admins never see it, and the screen tells the person so.
- **A different Finance:** the **Finance** permission in Admin, People is a staff job. It approves bounty payouts and reviews money orders. It does not open anyone''s Finance app.
- **Turning it on:** use the Flags tab to switch Finance for everyone, a share of people or one person.
- **Their data:** a person''s Finance lists are in their own export, and they go when the account is deleted.

> **Pro tip:** tell a new user to set the currency before logging anything. Riya then sees one clean total instead of a list of entries that are not added in.

> **Result:** at the end of the month Riya can see what came in, what went out, what she kept and what is still owed to her.

## The bottom line

Finance is a private money book. Pick the currency, log money in and out, note what is owed, and let what repeats post itself. Your job as an admin is only to keep the app available. What is inside is never yours to read.', 20, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l20', 'prog-admin-guide', 'The assistant, plans and credits', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya has a long day of edits. She wants to log her sleep, add a task to a pyramid and note a payment, without opening three apps. Bumling, the app''s own guide, does it for her from one panel.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What the assistant does for free and what needs a plan.
- How Riya picks a plan, reads her credits and undoes a change.
- How models and privacy work.
- How an admin starts a plan or adds credits.

## Open the assistant

The assistant is a round button with Bumling in it, at the bottom right of every screen in the app. On a phone it sits above the tab bar.

1. Riya taps the button.
2. A panel opens called **Bumling AI assistant**.
3. The page under it stays in view and updates as things change.

Close folds the panel back into the button and keeps the chat.

## The free half: flows

Flows are short forms that cost nothing. They write through the same steps a tap on a screen uses. Each one says where its answer lands.

Some of them:

- **Check in for today** and **Mark the small act done**, which land on the Daily tab.
- **Add to a pyramid**, which lands on that pillar in Pillars.
- **Mark my not to do list** and **Add to my not to do list**.
- **Log my sleep**, **Log my stress** and **Log mindful minutes**.
- **Add money in or out** and **Note money owed**, when Finance is on.
- **Write a review**, when Reviews is on.

A flow is hidden while its app is off. The screen under the panel picks the first few.

## The paid half: typing

Typing what you want, like "move my edit task to Friday", needs a plan.

1. Riya types a line in the box at the bottom.
2. Bumling does it in the app as Riya, with the same rules as her own taps.
3. Each change comes back as a row with **Undo**.
4. Removing something, or settling money owed, waits for her to tap **Confirm** or **Cancel**.
5. While it works, the send arrow turns into a **Stop** square. What was done before the stop stays, each with Undo.

Each reply says **Answered by** and the model''s name, so it is never mixed up with a built-in line.

Without a plan, she can still ask what a word means, like "what is a layer?". Anything else shows the plan card.

## Models and privacy

When a model is connected on the server, a model picker shows under the panel''s header. The list holds free models that can use the app''s tools.

- **A free model costs no credits**, but typing still needs a plan.
- **Some models keep nothing.** Only with one of those can the assistant read the journal and the trackers.
- **The journal is read only when asked.** The assistant reads it only when Riya asks about it.

The panel says under the picker what the chosen model may read.

## Pick a plan

1. Riya opens the Profile tab and taps **Plans and credits**.
2. She sees her **Plan** and what she **Used today**.
3. Under **Plans** she reads the three plans, all in rupees, each for 30 days:

- **Pro:** ₹1,499 for 10,000 credits.
- **Pro Max:** ₹12,499 for 100,000 credits.
- **Ultra:** ₹49,999 for 400,000 credits.

Plan credits that are left at the end do not carry over.

Under **Top up** are extra credits: ₹899 for 8,000, ₹2,299 for 20,000 and ₹4,499 for 40,000. They are used after the plan''s own and stay until used.

Payments are not live yet. Picking a plan or a top-up charges nothing, and the page says so. Until payments are live, the team switches plans on.

## Read the credits

- **One credit** is a tenth of a US cent of model use, worked out from the words the model read and wrote.
- **Credits used** shows each day for the last thirty days.
- **Limits by plan** shows what each agent may read and spend, with Riya''s column marked.
- **History** lists every credit in and out, newest first.

The Companion uses the same plan and credits. Without a plan, or once credits run out, it still answers free in its built-in way.

## What an admin does

You start plans by hand until payments are live.

1. Open Admin, then the **People** tab.
2. Search for Riya and tap her row.
3. Under **Plan and credits**, pick a plan and the number of days, then tap **Start**.
4. To add extra credits, type the number and tap **Add credits**. Add a line under "Why, for the history" if you like.
5. To end a plan, pick **No plan** and tap **End plan**. A sheet asks first. Plan credits go; extra credits stay.

You can also tick several rows and use **Start Pro** or **End plan** for all of them. Every change is written to the audit log.

## What an admin checks

- **The model key:** the model is set on the server. Until it is, the picker does not show.
- **The switch:** the assistant is on for everyone. The team can switch it off under Flags.
- **Its records:** the assistant keeps tool names, ids and short labels for Undo, never journal text.

> **Pro tip:** when you start a plan for a tester, add a few extra credits with a note. The note shows in their History, so they know where it came from.

> **Result:** Riya logs, plans and fixes things by typing one line, and every change has an Undo.

## The bottom line

The flows are free for everyone. Typing needs a plan, and credits pay for the model. Until payments go live, plans start from Admin, People, and every change you make there is in the audit log.', 21, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l21', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Admin: People', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya has been on the app for a few months. She helps new editors in Community, and the team wants her to give feedback in the editing program. That means a new job, maybe a badge and a plan. All of it happens in one place: Admin, People.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How to open Admin and find a person.
- What roles and permissions are, and how to give them.
- How to give xp, points, honors and badges.
- How to set what a person sees, start a plan and delete an account.

## Open Admin

Admin is in the Operations group, which only the team sees.

1. Open the Apps tab and open the Operations group.
2. Tap **Admin**.
3. Signed in as an admin, no key is needed. The cover says so.

The sections are tabs: **People, Listings, Redemptions, Orders, KYC, Life coaches, Flags, Discount codes, Attendee codes, Agency templates, Audit, Webhooks and Ops**. The row shows what fits, and **All** lists the rest.

Who is an admin? The founders'' email addresses are set on the server. Signing up or in with one of them gives the admin role, and that is written to the audit log.

## Find Riya

1. On the **People** tab, type her name, email, page or id in the search box.
2. Tap **Search**.
3. The table lists who matched. Sort by any column, filter, or page through.

You can change **Role** and **Plan** right in the row. Tap the row itself to open her panel.

## Roles and permissions

There are three roles:

- **Creator:** learns, works, connects and sells. Every new account is one.
- **Staff:** runs parts of the app, as far as their permissions go.
- **Admin:** everything, including roles, permissions and which features exist.

A permission is a job someone is trusted with. There are nine: **Talent manager, Cohort manager, Event host, Finance, Moderator, Support, Reviewer, Mentor and Life coach.**

- **A creator can hold three:** Mentor, Life coach and Moderator. The rest need the Staff role.
- **An admin holds all but Life coach.** Life coach is always given on purpose.
- **Only an admin changes a role,** and never their own.

## Give Riya a job

The team wants Riya to give feedback on assignments, and to help as a guide in Community.

1. Open her panel.
2. Under **Permissions**, tap **Mentor**. It turns on at once.
3. Tap **Moderator** too. Anyone with it is a guide in Community.
4. To take one away, tap it again. A sheet asks first.

Riya keeps the Creator role. She now gets the mentor tools in School and the guide tools in Community. The Manage screen is for staff only, so it stays closed to her.

## Give xp, points, honors and badges

Riya ran a free editing workshop, so the team thanks her.

1. Under **Honors**, pick one from the list, add a note if you like, and tap **Grant**. Tap an honor she holds to take it away; a sheet asks first.
2. Under **Points**, type an amount and tap **Add**.
3. Under **XP**, type an amount and tap **Add**. A minus number takes xp away, but never below zero.
4. Under **Badges given by hand**, pick a badge and tap **Give**.

A badge you gave shows on her profile and counts for unlocks and the leaderboard. Taking it away never touches a badge she earned herself.

## Start a plan or add credits

Payments are not live yet, so plans start here.

1. Under **Plan and credits**, pick a plan and the number of days.
2. Tap **Start**.
3. To add extra credits, type a number and tap **Add credits**, with a short reason.

## What does this person see?

1. Tap **What does this person see?** at the foot of her panel.
2. Every app is listed as a tick row.
3. Untick an app to block it for her. Tick it to allow it.
4. **Reset** removes what you set, and the usual rules decide again.

A struck-through app is one she is not allowed. A line under it says why: blocked for her, needs a higher role, not rolled out to her yet, or switched off for everyone. Locked apps cannot be blocked, and the server refuses it too.

This only decides what she may use. She still turns her own apps on and off herself.

## Change many people at once

Tick several rows. The buttons that appear are **Make creator, Make staff, Make admin, Make life coach, Start Pro and End plan**. The ones that take power away ask first.

## Delete an account

Say Riya made a second account to test her page, and asks you to remove it.

1. Search for that account.
2. Tap the bin icon at the end of its row.
3. A sheet asks "Delete" and the name. Tap **Yes, delete it**, or **Keep this account**.

Their details and what they made go. Money records stay, without their name. It cannot be undone. An admin account, and your own, can never be deleted here.

## What an admin checks

- **The audit log:** every role, permission, honor, badge, xp, plan and delete is written to the **Audit** tab, with the before and after.
- **Role changes are watched:** every admin''s inbox hears about role changes.
- **Never shown:** the panel never shows anyone''s journal, wellbeing, Finance or Discover answers.

> **Pro tip:** give a permission, not a role, wherever you can. A creator with Moderator is a guide, and nothing else about their account changes.

> **Result:** Riya is now a mentor and a guide, has a thank-you badge, and sees the apps the team meant her to see.

## The bottom line

People is where you give jobs and thanks. Search, tap the row, and use the panel. Give the smallest power that does the job, and trust the audit log to keep a record of each change.', 22, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l22', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Admin: the queues', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya now sells a short editing course, reserved a Kreators tee with her points, wants her payouts and hosts a meet-up for editors. Each of those lands in a queue the team works through. This lesson walks one week of Riya''s life through every queue.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Where each queue lives and what each button does.
- Which ones ask first, and which cannot be undone.
- Where the help queue and Community tools sit outside Admin.

## Where the queues are

Most queues are tabs in Admin: **Listings, Redemptions, Orders, KYC, Life coaches, Discount codes, Attendee codes, Agency templates and Webhooks.** Every list is a table you can sort, search, filter and page through. Tick rows to act on many at once.

Staff without the Admin role use **Manage** instead. **Your queues** shows a tile for each permission they hold, with how many are waiting.

## Listings

Listings are what the public site shows on Explore and each feature page.

1. On the **Listings** tab, the buttons start with **New**: New event, New program, New tool and so on.
2. Make one, then turn **Live** on in its row to publish it.
3. Turn **Featured** on to lift it to the top.
4. Tick several and use **Publish, Hide, Feature or Unfeature**.

Brands make their own campaigns public from Army, so there is no New campaign here.

## Redemptions

Riya spends points on a Kreators tee. Gear stays a reservation until a drop ships, and her points are held until then.

1. Open **Redemptions**. The tab shows how many wait.
2. When the tee ships, tick her row and tap **Mark fulfilled**.
3. If it cannot ship, tap **Cancel and refund**. A sheet asks first, and her points go back.

Card games are not here. They unlock at once when redeemed.

## Orders and refunds

A buyer of Riya''s course asks for their money back.

1. Open **Orders** and search by order, payment, buyer or page.
2. Tap the row to see its timeline and the provider''s events.
3. Type a **Refund reason**.
4. Tap **Refund in full**. The sheet says the amount and asks again: **Yes, refund it** or **Keep the order**.

A refund cannot be undone. A money order becomes paid only after the payment provider''s signed message arrives. The **Webhooks** tab lists every one of those messages, and **Replay** runs one again.

## KYC

Riya wants payouts, so she fills **Payout details** on her Shop''s Customers screen.

1. Open **KYC**. Only the last digits of her PAN and bank account are stored.
2. Check that the bank account and PAN match her legal name.
3. If she should hear why, type a line under **Note to them** first.
4. Tap **Approve** or **Reject**.

She sees the note beside her payout details.

## Discount codes

Riya''s course launch gets a code from the team.

1. Open **Discount codes** and tap **Make a code**.
2. Under **What it takes off**, pick a percent or an amount.
3. Under **Where it works**, pick the places, like Shop products. Use **Only one item** to tie it to her course.
4. Set **Times it can be used**, **Times per person**, **First day** and **Last day**.
5. Add a **Note for the team** and tap **Make the code**.

The platform pays the discount, so Riya still gets her full price. A code''s word and worth never change; a new deal is a new code. **Switch off** stops one at once.

## Attendee codes

Riya hosts a Party meet-up. Guests claim xp with a code.

1. Open **Attendee codes**. It lists Party, Run and Circles events with guests, soonest first.
2. Set the **Xp each**, up to 50, until the code opens thirty minutes before the start.
3. Set a **Code**, or tick rows and tap **New random codes**.

Only the event''s own guests can use the code, once each. The host is told about your change.

## Life coaches

Riya''s friend asks to become a life coach.

1. Open **Life coaches**. **Asked to be a life coach** lists the asks.
2. Tick a row and tap **Make life coach**, or **Say no**.
3. Under **Life coaches**, **Take the badge** ends every link of theirs and tells their students.
4. **Link a coach and a student** offers **Ask the student** or **Link now**. Link now only if the student already agreed.
5. **Every link** lists them all, with **End**.

A student can have up to three coaches.

## Agency templates

These are house templates every Agency user can copy. A super admin saves one from their own paper with **Save as house template**. It never carries who it was for, and carries business details only when **Include my business details** is on. Turn **Active** off to retire one.

## The help queue

Help requests sit on the **Help** screen, under **Open tickets**, for anyone with the Support permission. Each ticket has **Send reply**.

## Community tools

Guides are anyone with the Moderator permission. On Community:

- **For guides** has the switch **Hold a new member''s first post until a guide says okay**. A held post waits with **Say okay**.
- A post''s More menu has **Pin** (up to three a topic), **Hide** and **Mute**.
- Hide and Mute ask first. You can only mute someone of a lower rank.

## What an admin checks

- **The Audit tab:** who changed what, with the before and after.
- **Ops:** funnels counted from the app''s own tables, and the **Email outbox**.

> **Pro tip:** clear Redemptions and KYC first each week. Those are the queues where a real person is waiting on points or money.

> **Result:** Riya''s tee ships, her refund is fair, her payouts are checked and her meet-up pays its guests.

## The bottom line

Each queue has one job and plain buttons. Anything that takes back money, points or power asks first, and every change lands in the audit log.', 23, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-admin-guide-l23', 'prog-admin-guide', 'Running the platform', 'lesson', NULL, 'Riya never sees most of what keeps the app running. She sees an app switch on, a page that loads, and a privacy card that asks once. Behind that, the team decides what is live, watches for trouble and keeps her data where it belongs.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How to switch an app on for everyone, a share of people or one person.
- How to read Observability, the build map and the Logs.
- Your duties for privacy: the privacy card, data requests and the Grievance Officer.
- What must never reach staff.

## Feature flags

Every app is a module with a switch the team controls. A person''s own on and off is only a preference. If the team switches an app off, the server refuses it, not only the screen.

1. Open Admin and the **Flags** tab. The heading is **Feature flags**.
2. Each app is a row: **Module, Group, On, Minimum role, Rollout % and Overrides**.
3. Turn **On** off to hide an app for everyone.
4. Set **Minimum role** so only staff or admins can open it.
5. Set **Rollout %** to show it to a steady share of people.

A change takes a few seconds to reach everyone.

## Try an app with Riya first

Say the team wants to test Run with a small group before everyone gets it.

1. Set Run''s **Rollout %** to 10.
2. Tap **Add override** on Run''s row.
3. Under **For**, pick **One person (user id)** and paste Riya''s id.
4. Set **Force on**, add **Expires in days** if it should end, and a **Reason**.
5. Tap **Save override**.

The **Overrides** list shows every one, with **Remove**. **For** can also be **A role** or **A brand (organization id)**. In People, **What does this person see?** writes the same kind of override for one person in one tap.

## Observability

Observability is in the Operations group. Staff and admins can open it. It never shows journals or wellbeing.

- **People in numbers:** every account that is not deleted.
- **People per app:** how many have each app on.
- **Games, Discover and Agency:** counts only, never names, answers or amounts.
- **The log, last 24 hours:** changes, refused requests and errors, counted.
- **People:** accounts, newest first. Only an admin changes a role from a row''s menu, and a sheet asks first.

## The build map

The **Build map** at the foot of Observability shows the state of every part of the app.

- **Green** is built and checked.
- **Yellow** needs a key.
- **Orange** is test mode, or not tried live.
- **Dashed** is not built.

**Where things live** lists the files to open when something needs changing. Read the map before you promise anyone a feature.

## Logs

Only a super admin opens Logs, because the lines name people.

1. On Observability, tap **Logs** at the top.
2. Search a path, message or name.
3. Pick **Every line, Errors, Refused or Changes**.
4. Pick a person, or **Everyone**, and an area.

Lines are kept for thirty days. A line never holds what someone wrote. The journal, wellbeing, the Companion, Discover and the assistant only ever leave a bare error line, with no person and no path.

## Alerts in your inbox

Every admin''s inbox hears about bursts of failed sign-ins, too many requests, server errors and role changes. Each kind comes at most once an hour. A copyright notice from the public site lands there too.

## The privacy card

A visitor and a new member see a small card called **Your privacy**. It asks once about three things: the tracked-link cookie, videos from YouTube and Vimeo, and marketing email.

- **All three start off.** **Reject all** and **Accept all** are equal, side by side.
- **Choose for each** opens the three switches.
- **Riya changes them any time** on the Profile tab, under **Privacy choices**. The footer''s **Privacy choices** button brings the card back.

If a browser sends Global Privacy Control, the tracked-link cookie is never set, even after an earlier yes. Marketing email goes only with a recorded yes.

## Data requests

Riya owns her data. On the Profile tab, **Your data** holds **Export my data**, **Erase everything** and **Delete my account**.

- **Each one asks first** in a sheet.
- **Each request is logged without a name.** Those rows are kept for three years.
- **You never do it for her by hand.** If she writes in, point her to that row. Deleting an account from Admin, People is the same erase.

## The Grievance Officer

India''s privacy law asks for a named person to take complaints. The business''s details, the Grievance Officer''s name and email among them, are set on the server by whoever deploys the app. They are never typed into the code.

1. Open the public **Contact** page.
2. Check that the Grievance Officer''s name and email show.
3. If a field says "not set yet", ask whoever deploys to set it before launch.

The privacy card points questions to that officer. Never state a reply time unless the business sets one. Never claim a certification, a compliance label or an uptime number in any copy.

## What never reaches staff

- **The journal and wellbeing:** never to brands, staff, the leaderboard or Club selection.
- **Discover answers and results:** only the owner sees them.
- **The Companion''s chats and the Finance app:** private to the person.
- **Agency clients, fees and notes:** owner only.
- **A life coach:** sees only what the student switched on, and never the journal.

> **Pro tip:** before you switch an app on for everyone, look at its card on the build map. If it says it needs a key, set the key first.

> **Result:** Riya gets new apps when they are ready, her choices stick, and her private things stay private.

## The bottom line

Flags decide what is live. Observability and Logs show what is happening. Privacy is a duty, not a setting: check the Contact page, let people manage their own data, and never look where staff do not belong.', 24, 1);
